WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.940
- Welcome, my name is Alan Gerber,

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.960
I'm the director of the Institution

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.250
for Social and Policy Studies,

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.261
and today's event is an
ISPS Democracy event.

00:00:17.261 --> 00:00:20.720
ISPS has been holding a
series of events this year,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.560
bringing together experts
from Yale and beyond

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.340
to shed light on key questions

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.760
concerning American politics,
governance, and democracy.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.170
This is the last event in
the ISPS Democracy Series

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.850
this academic year,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.240
and we'll continue with new
events this coming fall.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.270
Before we begin today's event,

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:46.080
I'd like to thank the organizers
who put everything together

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.330
and without whose help

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.600
we wouldn't be able to hold these events,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.670
that would be Pam Greene, Pam Lamonaca,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.520
Tory Bilski, and Limor Peer.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.400
A little bit about today's logistics,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.420
after introductions of the panelists,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:04.720
each panelist will talk
for about 10 minutes,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.233
followed by discussion and a
question-and-answer period,

00:01:09.384 --> 00:01:12.480
moderated by Steven Wilkinson.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.880
Please save your questions till the end,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.060
during the question and answer period,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.410
you will be able to use
the Raise Hand button

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.470
on the bottom of the Zoom screen,

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.180
you can also send questions
to the panelists via chat.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.360
Please keep your questions brief,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.730
this event is being recorded,

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.650
and if you need technical assistance,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:37.143
please email isps@yale.edu.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.880
So now I'd like to introduce

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.640
all of tonight's panelists at once,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.723
they will talk one after
another without interruption.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.420
Here are tonight's panelists,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.820
or this afternoon's
panelists, first, Elisa Celis.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.130
Elisa is assistant professor

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.170
of statistics and data science at Yale,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.350
she is a co-founder

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.890
of the Computation and
Society Initiative at Yale,

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.600
she's an expert on artificial
intelligence and ethics,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.510
and her research focuses on how algorithms

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:13.730
encode and perpetuate
social and economic biases.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:17.130
Her work has included
designing voting techniques

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.270
that allow for the election
of diverse committees,

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.320
including the implementation of,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.370
including the Implementations

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.150
of Fair Elections in Switzerland project,

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.390
which is what I believe
she'll be talking about,

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.020
or focusing her remarks on this afternoon.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.710
Our second speaker is Mark Gorton,

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.480
Mark is a founder and chairman

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.170
of Tower Research Capital LLC,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.710
one of the leading computerized
trading firms in the world,

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.380
Gorton advocates for livable streets,

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.280
alternative transportation,
and open government

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.130
as the founder and chairman of Open Plans,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.480
a nonprofit devoted to the
pursuit of smart planning

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.170
and civic engagement through
media and digital tools.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.410
He is the chairman of Reinvent Albany,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:06.330
a nonprofit focused on
transparency, accountability,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.910
and good government in New York State.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.860
He is a graduate of Yale University

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.040
with a BS in electrical engineering

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.750
and an MS in electrical
engineering from Stanford,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.300
as well as an MBA from the
Harvard Business School.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.083
Our third panelists is Hélène Landemore,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.960
Landemore is associate professor

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.980
of political science at Yale.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.897
Her new book, "Open Democracy:

00:03:28.897 --> 00:03:32.400
"Reinventing Popular Rule
for the 21st Century"

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.770
came out this fall at
Princeton University Press,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.780
the book discusses
democratic representation,

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.410
and how we could open up
our electoral institutions

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.260
to ordinary citizens,

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:49.200
including via what she
terms open mini-publics.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.730
Landemore's previous
book, "Democratic Reason,"

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:56.670
was awarded the 2015 David
and Elaine Spitz Prize

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.050
for best book in liberal
and/or democratic theory,

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:04.210
and the Ideas, Knowledge
and Politics Best Book Award

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.350
by the American Political
Science Association.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.410
Helene is a policy expert

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.790
engaged in several initiatives
to promote democracy

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.240
working with governments and
think tanks around the world.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.520
And our fourth speaker is Rob Richie,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.420
Richie is president and CEO of FairVote.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.110
Rob founded FairVote in 1992,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.490
Fair Vote is a nonprofit organization

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.510
that researches and
advocates election reforms

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:36.160
to increase voter turnout,
accountable governance,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.360
and fair representation.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.330
He has been involved in
introducing ranked-choice voting

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.080
in states and more than 20 cities,

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.400
he's a graduate of Haverford College,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.273
where he serves on its corporation.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.423
Our moderator is Steven Wilkinson,

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.200
Steven is the Nilekani Professor

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.150
of India and South Asian Studies,

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.210
and a professor of political science

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.240
and international affairs.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.930
He is currently the Henry R. Luce director

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:11.250
of the MacMillan Center for
International and Area Studies.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.540
Wilkinson is a member
of the American Academy

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.100
of Arts and Sciences,

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.970
and his research focuses on South Asia.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.280
His most recent book, "Army and Nation,"

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.790
examined India's success in
managing the colonial army

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.750
it inherited in 1947.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.597
He has previously published
"Votes and Violence:

00:05:29.597 --> 00:05:33.180
"Electoral Competition and
Ethnic Riots in India,"

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.490
which examines the roots of
communal violence in South Asia.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.400
That book was the winner
of the Woodrow Wilson prize

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.020
of the American Political
Science Association

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.030
for best book on government, politics,

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.910
and international affairs.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.990
So with that, let me turn
it over to our panelists

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.440
and our moderator,

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.403
our first speaker is Professor Celis.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:57.360
- Hello everyone,

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.610
thank you so much for
that introduction, Alan,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.110
it's really great to be
here as part of this series,

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.100
and with all these amazing panelists,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.810
I'm really excited about the conversation

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:09.163
that we'll be having today.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.340
So what I wanted to talk about today

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.990
is representation in multi-winner voting,

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:17.010
and in particular, a case study

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.700
of some work I was really
lucky to be a part of,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.700
which happened in
Switzerland in 2017, 2018.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.510
So what was going on there,

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.510
in Switzerland, there's different cantons,

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.770
so you think of a canton
kind of a like state,

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:37.730
and the canton of Vaud
had decided by a vote

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.180
to rewrite its constitution,

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.343
so it was completely
starting from scratch,

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.930
and of course, the next question

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.530
once you decide to rewrite
the constitution is,

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.940
who is actually going to
be doing that rewriting?

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:53.450
And there was a lot of concern

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.580
and a lot of debates in the public sphere

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:00.580
as to how one could ensure
that, in this rewriting process,

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:05.650
that interests of every
citizen in the canton of Vaud

00:07:05.841 --> 00:07:09.663
will be adequately represented
when rewriting these rules.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:16.960
So while there, I was
working at l'EFPL at the time

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.050
in the neighboring canton,

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.600
I was lucky to get connected
with a group of activists

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.580
and also various political
parties who were very interested

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.890
in the concept of finding
representative ways

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.460
to conduct these elections

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.160
that still adhere to some
of the usual principles

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.930
that we have when conducting an election

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.700
in terms of maximizing the number of votes

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.020
that we would have.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:48.100
So in a traditional,

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.110
what I would call a traditional

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.280
multi-winner voting election,

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.040
if you want to, say,
elect four candidates,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.250
maybe you have eight
candidates that are running

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:56.900
and you wanna elect four of them,

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.070
normally one person comes in,
they can cast their votes,

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.577
the next person comes in,
they cast their votes,

00:08:02.577 --> 00:08:06.380
and so on and so forth until
everyone has cast their votes.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:07.470
In multi-winner elections,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.440
often you get to vote
for more than one person,

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.590
so it might be a little bit
different than some elections,

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.750
oh, sorry, (indistinct)

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.400
than other elections that
you might be familiar with,

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.850
but one by one, everyone
casts their votes,

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.500
at the end of the day,
you just add them up,

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:26.880
see how many votes each
particular candidate had,

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.040
and once you do that,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.560
you sort those votes
from highest to lowest,

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.130
and traditionally, the top candidates,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.330
up to however many you need
for your committee, win,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.350
so if you're electing four candidates,

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:45.420
here are your four winners of
this mock cartoon election.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.200
So the problem,

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.170
sorry, I don't know what's
going on with my slides today,

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:54.010
so the problem with this
is that this can result

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:58.350
in very disproportionate
groups of candidates

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.860
that are elected with respect to criteria

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.860
that you might care of with
respect to our presentation,

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:07.523
such as gender or age or race or locality.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.750
So the question was,

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.850
how can we have an election

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.370
that still satisfies

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:19.300
some representation type of constraints?

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.580
So for example, one could have a criteria

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:25.500
that says we would want
equal, or roughly equal,

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.940
numbers of men and women to win.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:31.500
So if you have one criteria, such as this,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.890
and you can partition your candidates,

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.270
it's pretty easy to
know what you should do,

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:39.193
you just take, say,

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:44.440
you can, of course, satisfy
these constraints trivially

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.500
by taking any two women and any two men,

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.960
in this toy example,

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.550
but that's, of course,
not what you want to do,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.320
what you want to do is take the two women

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:53.240
that have the most votes

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:56.084
and the two men that have the most votes,

00:09:56.084 --> 00:09:57.610
and that seems reasonable, right?

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:00.430
You're satisfying this criteria,

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:02.830
but you're also adhering

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.720
to this prioritization
of the votes, right?

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:08.220
You're still speaking to what the people

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:09.800
are voting for, right?

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.110
So the goal overall in
these types of question

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:13.940
is select the committee
with the most votes

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.470
that still satisfies the
kind of diversity constraints

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.030
that you would care about,

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:21.123
the problem is that if you
have more than one criteria,

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:24.970
this becomes computationally hard,

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:26.950
and that's the perspective
that I approach it from,

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:28.660
my background is in computer science,

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.500
I'm in the statistics and
data science department

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.520
here at Yale,

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:35.600
and that's basically when I was able

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.520
to step in and play a role,

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.530
the question is, how can we
balance multiple criteria?

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.470
Because we care about representation

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.300
across many different dimensions,

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.320
and along with some colleagues,

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:49.930
I was able to work on this problem

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:53.220
and come up with some really
interesting technical solutions

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.460
in the vein of approximation algorithms

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:58.700
that help you come up with,

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.700
help you design this in a way that,

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.160
once you collect all of these votes,

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.990
you can do exactly what you wanted, right?

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.500
You satisfy all these criteria,

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.130
and conditioned on satisfying

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:09.420
all of the diversity and
representation criteria

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.543
that you care about,

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:14.260
you return a committee,

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:17.592
you produce a committee that
has the most number of votes,

00:11:17.592 --> 00:11:19.780
and so it was really
exciting to be a part of this

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:22.010
and actually be able to have

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.230
this type of solution implemented

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.910
and used in this particular election.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.010
Of course, that is just one piece, right?

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:34.270
So one piece was the design of
the approximation algorithm,

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:35.730
thinking of this computationally,

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:37.440
looking at the algorithm design,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:38.510
and it's a very interesting piece,

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:40.550
but it's just one part of the equation.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:43.350
I think a very large part of the equation,

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:47.170
which several of my co-panelists here

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:49.610
would be able to speak more to,

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.950
is what are the good ways

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:54.250
to design this process as a whole, right?

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:57.570
So who is even deciding what
are the right constraints?

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:59.160
What are the right categories?

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:02.850
How do we even decide what
is a representative sample?

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:04.540
And what we...

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:07.710
The process that was used

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:11.870
in this particular case
study that I'm talking about,

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:13.780
what we did was a two-stage process,

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:17.110
so first, well, two (indistinct)

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:18.990
process leading up to that

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:23.480
in terms of different community events,

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.040
a lot of discussion that
happened in the public realm,

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:30.030
but basically, when it came down to votes,

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.860
first, a set of constraints were proposed,

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:37.860
so should the committee be
representative across gender,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.170
should it representative across race,

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.690
should it representative
across religion, et cetera,

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:47.690
so different places where one
could enforce representation,

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.250
so first, one voted on the criteria

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:52.870
and what that would look like,

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:57.640
and then about a month later
was the actual election

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.750
that ran just to vote on
the candidates themselves,

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:02.890
so that's how this
particular election worked,

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:03.960
although there would, of course,

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.200
be many potentially different ways

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:09.833
to design this process and to
elicit those initial votes.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.560
In this particular case,
there was, of course,

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:17.490
a canton as a whole,

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:22.100
but there were different regions

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.710
within each canton that were
each having an election,

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:26.240
so it was really kind of eight elections

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:27.330
that ran in parallel,

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:30.210
and the biggest one was
a committee of size 18

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.380
that was being elected from 50 candidates,

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:36.083
and each voter could
vote for 18 candidates,

00:13:36.083 --> 00:13:41.083
and the type of criteria that
were elicited via this process

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.130
that they agreed upon was one for gender,

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:46.220
one for age, and one for locality,

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.550
so there's a lot of political tension

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:50.710
between people living in cities

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:52.810
and people living in the countryside,

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:55.380
so that was one of the places

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:59.450
in which diverse
representation was required

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.210
in this election,

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:03.840
and then once that was satisfied,

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:05.850
we could use our type of technical

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.150
for a multi-winner voting solution.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:09.900
So I just wanted to
present this case study,

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:12.193
I think there's, again, many
different people on the panels

00:14:12.193 --> 00:14:13.900
that can speak to different parts

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:15.333
of this process as a whole,

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.770
and I look forward to
hearing your questions,

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:21.500
and I'm happy to dive into
any more of the details

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.980
on the different aspects of this.

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:27.640
On the technical side, I
worked with Lingxaio Huang,

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:30.380
Vijay Keswani, and Nisheeth Vishnoi,

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.640
and then our partners in particular

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.630
was a nonprofit called Appel Citoyen,

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:37.520
with Florian Evequoz and Johan Rochel,

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.230
so I just wanted to say my
thank you to them as well,

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:43.460
it was really a fun and exciting
project to be a part of,

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:46.420
and I'm happy to share with you.

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:47.253
Thank you.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.063
- Hi, I'm Mark Gorton.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:56.800
I'd like to first thank Alan
for inviting me to participate,

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:58.350
and everyone who's put this together,

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:02.360
it's really an honor for me
to be included in this group

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.300
and be asked to talk.

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:08.870
I'm gonna speak about the
problems with mass elections

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:10.337
and present what I call

00:15:10.337 --> 00:15:13.473
the micro-democracy/working
group alternative.

00:15:17.856 --> 00:15:20.273
So I have been quite involved

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:27.010
in politics or civic engagement

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.870
in New York City and New
York State for quite a while,

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:34.090
and I have seen

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.960
the structural problems that exist,

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.060
and it seems like good government groups

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:42.020
are always fighting a system

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:47.020
where the entrenched interests

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:49.750
are working for themselves

00:15:50.788 --> 00:15:53.980
and not in what you would
think of the interests

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:57.180
of the general public,

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.010
and after fighting time and again

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:01.520
and through election cycle again,

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:06.520
it just seemed like the
structure of the system

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.700
was so fundamentally flawed

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:12.420
that I began to think

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:17.050
that the problem comes
down to the very nature

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:18.840
of mass elections,

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:21.860
and I'm sure a lot of
people are very aware

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:23.790
with lots of problems that we have

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:27.623
with poor-quality leaders
and corruption in the system,

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:33.030
and I just see time again

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:34.990
the corrupting nature of elections

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:38.110
as candidates are
required to make promises,

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:41.970
get money, build coalitions,

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:45.710
and so, frequently, what you
have is sort of good people

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:49.480
who are forced to corrupt
themselves just to get elected,

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:51.550
and that creates

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:55.340
all sorts of issues

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.170
and all sorts of corruptions and problems

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.310
we have in our government,

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.840
and I'm sure I don't
have to tell people here

00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:04.543
about lots of these.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:07.160
So I started thinking

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:11.110
about how it would be possible to correct,

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:15.920
to build an alternative
system that both has,

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:19.030
is representative and
captures democratic intent,

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:22.190
but also is resistant to corruption

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.410
and attracts and selects good leaders,

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:27.880
and after thinking about it,
I have formulated some ideas,

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:30.380
which I will share here,

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:35.380
so it's basically based
on a working-group system,

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:40.670
so rather than having mass elections

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:45.670
where everybody in the population
is expected to participate

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:48.620
in a very, very thin and trivial way

00:17:48.620 --> 00:17:51.020
in lots and lots of elections,

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:53.740
and, as most people
know, in most elections,

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:56.663
your vote really somehow
doesn't matter very much.

00:17:57.819 --> 00:18:00.240
The concept of democratic participation

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:04.170
would be shifted towards
one that is much chunkier

00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:08.010
of occasionally participating
in a working group

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:13.010
that would be taking a
very specific decision,

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:17.570
so rather than having a small say

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:19.430
in lots and lots of decisions,

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:21.120
really, this focused attention

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.760
is partially designed
to get a better outcome.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:28.430
So I imagine a three-step process,

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:32.820
so the first step is an election,

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.800
and it's actually, but
it's a micro-election,

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:39.760
a very, very small election among a group

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.060
of perhaps somewhere between
40 and 100 neighbors,

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:48.060
so this election is meant
to be not very daunting,

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:50.690
easy to campaign for,

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:54.830
among people that are hopefully well-known

00:18:57.983 --> 00:18:59.340
to the candidates,

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:04.200
and the reason that I had thought

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:06.280
that having these
micro-elections would be good

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:09.390
is because the fact is that most people

00:19:11.119 --> 00:19:16.040
are not very engaged in
politics and civic events,

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:20.960
and rather than, you know,
some people are just too busy,

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:21.810
don't have time,

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:25.100
so rather than trying to get people

00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:28.920
who are not particularly
engaged to be engaged,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.610
that would have a chance to get people

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:34.690
who could be representative
of their own community

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:37.540
and who would care to be engaged,

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:40.160
and still having these
very small elections

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:44.120
would give you a fairly
good representative sample

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:46.130
of the population,

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:50.870
so basically, at the end of
this process of micro-elections,

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:53.550
about 1% of the population

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.200
would be chosen as micro-representatives

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:58.430
or people that would be available

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:00.733
to serve on working groups later.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:07.110
So then the next step would
be staffing working groups,

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:10.950
and this would be done randomly,

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:13.760
so really, these working groups would work

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:18.760
much more similarly to the
way juries function right now

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:23.420
where people are chosen at
random to participate in a jury,

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:27.290
this, people would be chosen from random

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:29.370
of the pool of micro-representatives

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:32.190
to participate in these working groups,

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:37.110
and then the process that I imagine

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:43.030
for choosing office-holders
would be decomposed,

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:47.910
and the example that I'm presenting here

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:51.670
is something that I
think of as an election

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:55.010
for a reasonable-size office,

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:57.650
so maybe a member of the
House of Representatives

00:20:57.650 --> 00:20:59.980
or a governor of a moderate-sized state

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:02.170
or a mayor of a city,

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:03.780
this process could be scaled,

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.810
I mean, obviously, a process
that involves 100 people

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:11.010
is too much to choose members
of a local school board

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:13.580
and maybe too few to choose

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:15.330
the president of the United States,

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:18.980
but still, these process can be scaled

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:21.670
for the appropriate size,

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.533
but the important thing

00:21:23.533 --> 00:21:28.360
are the steps that I have
decomposed it to here,

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:31.660
so the three steps are

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:35.320
choosing the candidates to be considered,

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:38.220
doing research on those candidates,

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:42.160
and then finally, choosing
the final office holder,

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:45.860
and a big part of the reason

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.840
that I am suggesting breaking it up

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:53.820
into different job functions

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:56.293
is to reduce the risk of corruption.

00:21:57.340 --> 00:21:58.730
I think it's quite possible,

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:02.120
again, particularly for large
and high-stakes elections,

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.550
that having a small number of people,

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:07.643
I mean, maybe as a few as 100

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:12.830
who all a very strong say

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:17.830
in the process of choosing
the final office holder,

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:21.620
those people obviously
could be easily targets

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:24.160
of bribes and threats
and things like that,

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:25.890
again, if you're talking about choosing

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:27.220
the mayor of a small town,

00:22:27.220 --> 00:22:29.410
maybe these are not really issues,

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:31.410
for much bigger offices,

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.760
much more so for if you're talking

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:34.890
about choosing the president,

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.140
you might wanna choose the people

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:38.970
and quickly sequester them away

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:40.743
so they couldn't be corrupted,

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:47.210
but really, a big part of why I think

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:51.390
this could be a superior solution

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:56.390
is that you have people who have the time

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:59.230
and a reason to focus,

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:04.090
a reason to invest time and effort

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:06.660
in really becoming educated

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:09.970
about the people who are involved,

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:12.130
putting in time to be thoughtful,

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:15.010
one of the great challenges
with mass elections

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:17.660
is that you have so many people

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:19.950
who have so little stake in the system

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:24.900
that it's very hard for them
to merit investing the time

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.240
to be making an informed decision,

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.990
so by having these much more engaged,

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:32.090
informed, deliberative bodies,

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:34.913
the hope is that the,

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:41.600
the choice that will be made
will be much more rational

00:23:43.236 --> 00:23:45.660
and you have people who
are much more engaged,

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.250
and a lot of the
techniques that we see now

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:51.140
in terms of mass disinformation

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:52.687
and these social media campaigns,

00:23:52.687 --> 00:23:54.980
you know, all the different things

00:23:55.940 --> 00:24:00.060
that are distorting the
democratic process now,

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:02.680
this process that I'm proposing

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:06.110
is somewhat immune, or at
least resistant, to that.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:11.110
Now, there are other corrupting vectors

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:13.620
that could be used to attack this,

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:15.480
but I do think that the fact

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:20.410
that you bring random people together

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:22.510
for a brief period of time

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:27.430
then disband these
committees when they're done

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:31.590
means that the target

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:33.310
for any sort of corrupting attack

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:34.710
is really ephemeral,

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:39.200
and it can be that hopefully
would make this whole system

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:43.130
very resistant to corruption,
or at least fairly resistant,

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:46.390
which I think is really
one of the main things

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:50.003
that I've been shooting for
in my design of this system.

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:55.970
So thank you very much for
taking the time to listen to me,

00:24:55.970 --> 00:24:58.930
I, as opposed to everyone
else involved here,

00:24:58.930 --> 00:25:02.030
I'm an amateur, I'm not
involved in academia,

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:05.080
but I really am interested
in working on these ideas,

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:08.930
so if anyone is interested in engaging,

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:10.770
I'd be really happy to talk to people,

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:13.253
here is my information,

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:18.100
and I hope to be able to
make some connections,

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:19.323
so thank you very much.

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:24.570
- Hi everyone, this is my turn,

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.580
I'm Hélène Landemore,

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:28.980
I'm not going to use a PowerPoint

00:25:30.010 --> 00:25:31.880
because I suspect that the reason

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:33.860
I don't need a PowerPoint is that my idea

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:36.793
is much simpler than what's
been presented so far.

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:39.900
Instead of offering what I see

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:43.480
as complicated epicycles to
salvage the current system,

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:46.740
I propose changing the system altogether.

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:47.573
The main problem I see

00:25:47.573 --> 00:25:52.180
with what we call political
representation today,

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:54.560
as I had diagnosed in my
book, "Open Democracy,"

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:57.340
is that this type of representation

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:00.400
and the regime form it corresponds to

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:01.900
is fundamentally exclusionary,

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:08.160
electoral representation,
as Aristotle, Montesquieu,

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:10.110
and then Brennan more recently

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.003
have told us over and over again,

00:26:12.883 --> 00:26:16.230
is an aristocratic selection mechanism

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:20.323
based on an in-egalitarian
principle of distinction.

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:22.503
Why?

00:26:22.503 --> 00:26:25.830
Because elections only empower
those who stand out socially

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:27.020
in the eyes of most,

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:29.220
this is why the Greeks of classical Athens

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.120
did not use elections to
staff political offices,

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:35.190
instead, they used a lottery-based system

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.120
that distributed power equally

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:41.390
among the individuals who
qualified as citizens.

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:44.610
Citizenship was defined in
exclusionary ways, it's true,

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:45.710
in ancient Greece,

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:48.140
but distribution of political
influence among citizens

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:51.060
was perfectly inclusive at a given time.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:53.880
We modeled, by contrast,
we invented democracy

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:55.490
in a very elitist form,

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:58.650
choosing elections as
the primary mechanism

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:00.100
to select representatives,

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:04.000
meaning to select people who
are going to stand for us

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:07.470
and make decisions on our behalf,

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:08.830
and even as the franchisers

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:11.680
kept expanding since the 18th century

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:12.560
and the restrictions

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:15.080
on who can run for
office have disappeared,

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:17.720
access to power in electoral democracies

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:21.410
remains limited to those
who can win elections,

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:22.880
again, because they have attributes

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:25.470
that are not readily
distributed in the population,

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:28.830
say, charisma, height, intelligence,

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:30.900
but also money and connections.

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:34.250
Meanwhile, other people's power,

00:27:34.250 --> 00:27:38.900
perhaps 95%, 98% of
the rest of the people,

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:42.447
is limited to those enfranchised
people every four years.

00:27:42.447 --> 00:27:44.603
"So why is that a problem?" you might ask.

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:47.210
Well, not only does it mean

00:27:47.210 --> 00:27:49.780
that we do not live in
authentic democracies,

00:27:49.780 --> 00:27:50.970
since for most of us,

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:53.390
representative democracy means being ruled

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:55.660
and almost never ruling,

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:57.980
but electoral democracies tend to suffer

00:27:57.980 --> 00:27:58.970
from the blind spots

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:02.260
one would typically expect
from oligarchic regimes.

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:05.580
They have a tendency to ignore,
among others, their poor,

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.760
their young, their minorities of color,

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:09.490
and the losers, they can't make policies

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:12.340
like free trade or austerity measures.

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:13.970
This is so because these people

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:16.780
rarely make it to
positions of elected power,

00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:19.310
or any power at all, for that matter.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:21.210
Yet, if these people are not in the room

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:24.170
where laws and policies get
deliberated on and made,

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:27.820
not only do their interests
fail to be taking into account,

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:29.670
but even if they are taken into account,

00:28:29.670 --> 00:28:32.450
such interests are not as
likely to be served properly

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:35.240
for lack of a proper
first-hand perspective

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.720
applied to explaining and promoting them.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:40.780
In the U.S., these blind
spots of electoral regimes

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:43.540
have harmed the welfare of
the African-American community

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:45.890
since the birth of American democracy,

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:48.030
and more recently, the
welfare of the working class

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:50.543
since the rise of
neoliberalism in the 1980s.

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:53.760
In France, similarly,
a government consisting

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:56.340
of largely urban, educated individuals

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:59.110
who live in city center
and perhaps bike to work,

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:02.820
were shocked to discover that
the well-intentioned fuel tax

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:04.330
in November of 2018

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:07.120
could generate the
sometimes violent pushback

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:09.353
of peri-urban, lower middle-class people,

00:29:09.353 --> 00:29:12.203
causing the rebellion now
known as the yellow vests.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:16.230
This elitist, biased electoral
democracy cannot be fixed,

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:17.900
it is intrinsic to elections

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:19.250
and they get worse with time

00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:22.660
or under conditions of
growing economic inequalities,

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:24.910
it can be mitigated,
but it cannot be fixed.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:28.130
My solution consists in finally
facing up to this reality,

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:29.220
to this fact,

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:31.890
and considering alternative
modes of representation

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:34.700
that are more inclusive and egalitarian,

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:38.340
namely, more democratic in
the proper sense of the term.

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:42.140
The innovation here is both
conceptual and institutional,

00:29:42.140 --> 00:29:46.187
I propose to decouple the idea
of democratic representation

00:29:46.187 --> 00:29:49.030
and the idea of electoral representation,

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:52.010
these are not synonymous,

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:55.100
or in other words, I propose
to render elections optional

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:58.200
to the concept of
democratic representation.

00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:00.513
Second, I develop a division of democracy

00:30:00.513 --> 00:30:03.110
that I call open democracy,

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:04.300
partly based on this new,

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:06.870
non-electoral concept of representation,

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:08.850
and I argue that only in such a regime

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:10.760
would we be truly political equals

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:12.160
and able to avail ourselves

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:15.070
as the entire collective
intelligence of society.

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:16.730
An open democracy is structured

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:19.010
by five institutional principles,

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:21.300
participatory rights, deliberation,

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:22.673
the majoritarian principle,

00:30:22.673 --> 00:30:24.873
democratic representation,
and transparency,

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:27.250
but here is not the place

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:29.720
to develop the full
vision for open democracy,

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:31.580
so let me simply focus

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:34.240
on this idea of democratic representation

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:37.160
as distinct from electoral representation,

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:39.860
and on the core institution
of an open democracy

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:43.670
that this democratic
representation translate into,

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:45.383
namely, the open mini-public.

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:50.080
A central contender for a type
of political representation

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:53.310
that is inclusive and
accessible to all equally

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:56.700
is indeed what I call
lottocratic representation,

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:59.260
that's representation
based on random selection,

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:01.070
or selection by lot.

00:31:01.070 --> 00:31:02.790
Examples of lottocratic bodies

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:06.750
are the Council of 500
of classical Athens,

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:09.310
as well as these lots in our juries,

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:10.990
and under modern circumstances,

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:14.420
things like citizen juries,
consensus conferences,

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:18.660
deliberative polls, citizens'
assemblies, and the like,

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:20.990
all qualify as type of mini-publics

00:31:20.990 --> 00:31:23.520
because they aim to present

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:26.220
a miniature portrait of the people,

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:29.550
although technically, this is
only true of very large ones.

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:31.890
So what if, instead of elected chambers,

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:33.730
we used randomly selected ones

00:31:33.730 --> 00:31:37.290
to set the agenda for the
polity, or even make laws?

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:39.640
The advantage of random
selection is twofold,

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:41.060
first, it creates a much more egalitarian

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:43.040
distribution of power,

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.870
if we institutionalize such bodies

00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:46.280
at all levels of the polity,

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:49.057
and perhaps also in firms,
hospitals, and schools, say,

00:31:49.057 --> 00:31:50.650
and with enough rotation,

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:52.930
all of us would have an equal
chance, and a real chance,

00:31:52.930 --> 00:31:56.350
of exercising real power
over the course of our life,

00:31:56.350 --> 00:32:01.167
not just consenting to
it at regular intervals,

00:32:01.167 --> 00:32:04.400
and if this is true, I think
even if we need to implement

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:07.380
stratified random sampling
rather than pure sortition,

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:08.980
because for smaller bodies,

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:12.390
this is the only way to get
the categories we care about

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:14.340
represented somewhat fairly.

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:16.660
It is, in that sense, a
democratic improvement

00:32:16.660 --> 00:32:17.980
over elected parliaments,

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:19.730
where most of us, we never set foot

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:24.120
Second, a random selection
or stratified random sampling

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:26.590
reproduces in small
the cognitive diversity

00:32:26.590 --> 00:32:27.800
of the larger group,

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.340
namely, the different ways of thinking

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:31.380
present in the population,

00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:34.110
this minimizes the risk of blind spots

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:35.400
and increases our chances

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.030
of making good decisions for the group.

00:32:38.030 --> 00:32:39.860
I thus propose as the heart

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:43.860
of a considerably reformed
democracy the open mini-public,

00:32:43.860 --> 00:32:45.520
a body of a few hundred people

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:49.730
forming a new kind of
representative legislative body,

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:52.410
this body is open, I specify,

00:32:52.410 --> 00:32:54.120
because in order to avoid closure

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:56.280
and disconnection with the larger public,

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:58.440
its deliberation must remain accessible

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:00.460
and visible to the rest of the public,

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:02.210
including interest groups,

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:05.430
who can input those
deliberations at any stage,

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:09.060
for example, via the use of
online crowdsourcing platform.

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:10.770
This allows the smaller body

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:12.990
to tap the information
of the larger public,

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:15.230
but also promotes a larger public debate

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:18.570
about the nature and content
of the deliberation going on,

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:21.060
which is important for the
legitimacy of the mini-public

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:22.600
to begin with.

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:24.410
So it's highly unlikely at this stage

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:27.070
that a legislative open
mini-public of this kind

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:29.660
is going to replace elected assemblies,

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:32.150
though some have been calling
for something like this,

00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:35.650
and I personally think that
this is the more coherent ideal,

00:33:35.650 --> 00:33:37.260
as I mentioned.

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:40.550
Again, at this stage, what
we can realistically consider

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:42.897
is augmenting electoral democracy

00:33:42.897 --> 00:33:46.330
and electoral assemblies
with such lottocratic bodies

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:48.060
to make them more truly,

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:51.103
to make the whole system
more truly representative.

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:53.560
Even if no replacement occurs,

00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:56.430
though, again, some people
have called, for example,

00:33:56.430 --> 00:34:00.557
for replacing the Canadian
senate with a lottocratic body,

00:34:02.149 --> 00:34:04.986
or the House of Lords
with a lottocratic body,

00:34:04.986 --> 00:34:07.950
or the (speaks French) in
France with a lottocratic body,

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:09.080
the change still implies

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:12.437
a significant redistribution
of power from elected chambers

00:34:12.437 --> 00:34:15.020
to the new open mini-publics.

00:34:15.020 --> 00:34:18.010
One could imagine having
an openly public legislate

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:20.110
specifically about environmental issues,

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:24.430
issues around climate change
or issues about the future,

00:34:24.430 --> 00:34:27.840
and we could also envisage giving them

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:29.360
the power to veto certain bills

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:30.650
coming from the other chambers

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:33.780
when they run against it's own mission.

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:35.960
or one could imagine ad hoc mini-publics

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:37.500
being convened on certain issues

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:40.060
at the initiative of either
the government, parliament,

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:42.620
or the citizen themselves by other right

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.343
known as citizen initiative.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:46.193
In the latter case,

00:34:46.193 --> 00:34:48.100
the recommendations of
the one-off mini-public

00:34:48.100 --> 00:34:49.920
would have to be made at
least somewhat binding

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:51.630
on the other legislative institutions

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:52.950
because we know from experience

00:34:52.950 --> 00:34:55.610
otherwise, they largely get ignored.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:58.300
This proposal, even in its more reformist

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:01.460
and complementary version
may seem farfetched to you,

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:04.410
but in fact, a lot of
experimentations around similar ideas

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:08.333
have been underway for over 20 years.

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:12.710
A recent OECD report titled,
"The Deliberative Wave"

00:35:12.710 --> 00:35:17.510
has documents around 287
deliberative experiments

00:35:17.510 --> 00:35:21.020
with all the mini-publics of
that kind around the world

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:22.370
over the last 20 years,

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:26.920
and just in the last decade,
there's been a surge as well.

00:35:26.920 --> 00:35:28.390
Among the more salient example,

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:31.860
I'll just mention, in 2017 in South Korea,

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:35.070
a citizens' assembly of
randomly selected citizens,

00:35:35.070 --> 00:35:36.410
which was given the final say

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:38.610
over the decision whether or not

00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:41.593
to continue the construction
of two nuclear reactors,

00:35:42.450 --> 00:35:44.410
a decision that was actually implemented

00:35:44.410 --> 00:35:47.720
over the country president's
preferred choice.

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:50.830
In 2017 as well, Mongolia passed a law

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:53.000
requiring that a deliberative poll be held

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:54.380
prior to constitutional amendments.

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:56.950
In late 2018 in Ireland,

00:35:56.950 --> 00:35:59.580
a popular referendum
validated the recommendations

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:02.120
of a specially convened citizens' assembly

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:05.110
of 99 randomly selected citizens

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:07.440
to decriminalize abortion,

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:09.330
and this was followed by a referendum

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:11.550
in which this proposal approved.

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:12.950
Most recently in France,

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:14.750
the Citizens' Convention for Climate,

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:18.220
which I've studied and closely observed,

00:36:18.220 --> 00:36:21.260
made 149 recommendations about how to curb

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:24.960
greenhouse gas emissions in
a spirit of social justice,

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:26.130
half of those proposals

00:36:26.130 --> 00:36:28.310
have now been turned into regulations,

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:30.280
and many of them are currently

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:34.923
influencing various bills.

00:36:37.674 --> 00:36:39.980
At the smaller scale,
regions are now interested

00:36:39.980 --> 00:36:41.850
in using mini-publics to set the agenda

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:43.110
of the legislative bodies,

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:44.730
as has been the case for a year

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:47.140
in the German-speaking region
of Belgium, for example,

00:36:47.140 --> 00:36:49.783
where they had a council of
24 randomly selected citizens,

00:36:49.783 --> 00:36:52.610
a very small jury-like body

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:55.900
that could convene larger
citizens' assemblies

00:36:55.900 --> 00:36:59.120
and set the agenda for
the local parliament.

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:00.690
Brussel, the city of Brussels

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:03.010
now is developing so-called
deliberative committees

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.780
to help guide public policy.

00:37:04.780 --> 00:37:05.810
At the global level,

00:37:05.810 --> 00:37:08.420
engineers and academics
are now organizing,

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:09.700
it's coming up this fall,

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:11.440
the first global climate assembly

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:13.120
to accompany the negotiations

00:37:13.120 --> 00:37:17.740
around the Conference of the
Parties in 2021 in Glasgow.

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:19.400
So I'm looking forward
to seeing more of this

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:21.520
being tried out in the
United States as well,

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:24.930
I believe this is the only way to aim

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:28.920
for a more democratic kind
of political representation.

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:29.920
Thank you very much.

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:34.620
- Thank you, it's terrific
to be part of this,

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:35.603
I'm Rob Richie.

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:41.320
I often feel I'm talking about change

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:43.140
in a way that's bolder than most people,

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:45.670
but I feel in this set of presentations,

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:47.510
maybe I'm being moderate

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:50.780
by only being for proportional voting

00:37:50.780 --> 00:37:52.170
for U.S. House elections

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:54.430
and widespread use of
ranked-choice voting.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:57.780
I will say there's
something important to me

00:37:57.780 --> 00:37:59.530
about the electoral process

00:37:59.530 --> 00:38:01.950
that doesn't displace some
of these other bold ideas,

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:04.530
but also means that the seeking
consent of the governed,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:06.390
reaching out to people

00:38:06.390 --> 00:38:09.770
in a process where there's full debate

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:12.170
is something that I
certainly wanna make sure

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:16.210
stays part of any system that we have.

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:17.510
I'll go ahead and share my screen

00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:21.123
and get into my PowerPoint here.

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:27.320
So what I'm gonna zero in on

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:32.310
is those ideas of what
we can do statutorily

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:33.730
in the United States,

00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:35.610
and I wanna get at it through the lens

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:40.290
of why it is particularly important now.

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:41.870
We are in time of sort of heightened,

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:44.700
deep political polarization,

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:49.260
and to us, it's really grounded

00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:51.500
in the use of winner-take-all voting,

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:56.280
so election rules drive behavior,

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:57.480
we don't think this is,

00:38:57.480 --> 00:39:00.420
what we're seeing in Congress
and in many of our elections

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:04.530
is because of bad people
who decide to run,

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:08.230
but they have certain incentives
that drive what happens.

00:39:08.230 --> 00:39:10.440
You can certainly see in very basic ways

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:12.910
that the election rules drive behavior in,

00:39:12.910 --> 00:39:14.090
just use a couple examples,

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:16.340
when you're having presidential
nomination processes,

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:17.940
Iowa goes first,

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:21.200
and lo and behold, about 50% of events

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:24.140
in the first run-up to the year

00:39:24.140 --> 00:39:26.930
before the nomination contests take place

00:39:26.930 --> 00:39:28.720
take place in Iowa.

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:31.670
The swing states in the
general election for president

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:33.860
are the ones that are tightly contested,

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:35.390
others are not,

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:39.230
and about 98% of campaign events and ads

00:39:39.230 --> 00:39:42.040
are earmarked for voters
in those 12 states,

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:43.430
not because the candidates don't care

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:44.370
about the rest of the voters,

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:48.470
but it doesn't affect
whether they win or lose.

00:39:48.470 --> 00:39:51.520
So winner-take-all means 51% of votes,

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:52.460
which, of course, is a majority,

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:57.340
wins 100% of representation, 49% wins 0,

00:39:57.340 --> 00:39:59.960
and if it gets a little bit more tilted,

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:03.460
55% or so, it's essentially a done deal,

00:40:03.460 --> 00:40:05.370
that if you're comfortably ahead

00:40:05.370 --> 00:40:07.490
or hopelessly behind, you can't win.

00:40:07.490 --> 00:40:09.410
So there's a good book by Lee Drutman

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:10.320
called "The Doom Loop,"

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:12.550
and I sort of walk through
how things are playing out,

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:15.100
but we're in a cycle
that's reinforcing itself

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:16.660
and things are getting worse and worse,

00:40:16.660 --> 00:40:19.200
so first, most states and
districts have a partisan tilt,

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:24.200
that's natural that the parties
aren't so evenly placed,

00:40:24.230 --> 00:40:26.380
I mean, as political
polarization increases,

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:28.560
that winner-take-all rule
means that partisan majorities

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:30.320
decide more and more outcomes,

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:32.420
general election doesn't really matter.

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:35.290
So to win the election,

00:40:35.290 --> 00:40:36.460
most candidates don't really need

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:38.060
to worry about the general election,

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:40.240
they only need to worry
about how they get nominated

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:42.320
to be part of the majority
party in that area,

00:40:42.320 --> 00:40:43.550
so they only need to cater

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:46.216
to those relatively small
percentage of voters

00:40:46.216 --> 00:40:47.770
that participate in partisan primaries,

00:40:47.770 --> 00:40:48.740
there was a recent study,

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:51.510
it's about 10% of voters
are really deciding

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.163
the great majority of outcomes.

00:40:55.358 --> 00:40:56.890
And for some reason...

00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:57.830
Okay, there we go.

00:40:57.830 --> 00:41:00.100
And then as a result,

00:41:00.100 --> 00:41:01.810
Congress is now more prone to gridlock,

00:41:01.810 --> 00:41:04.680
and you can see just how
difficult it is for the parties

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:08.160
to take on anything
substantive and challenging

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:09.960
and actually do things together,

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.970
their base doesn't want them
to do it, they're fighting,

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:16.090
they can get things done when
one party runs everything,

00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:17.420
but even that can be challenging,

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:18.690
but certainly, when they're divided,

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:20.100
it gets really challenging,

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:23.120
these disputes lead to
greater partisan ill will,

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:25.900
the partisan voters therefore
become more fearful and angry

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:27.350
of the base,

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:28.880
and so the cycle continues,

00:41:28.880 --> 00:41:31.960
and so things just keep
getting worse and worse.

00:41:31.960 --> 00:41:34.053
So how can we sort of get out of this?

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:36.360
We first have to start with the fact

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:38.800
that we're in a very difficult place

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:40.470
in a winner-take-all election system,

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:42.360
more than 80% of congressional races

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:43.990
are just fundamentally safe

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:46.250
because of that winner-take-all dynamic.

00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:48.180
FairVote does a report

00:41:48.180 --> 00:41:50.170
we started way back about 25 years ago

00:41:50.170 --> 00:41:51.800
called "Monopoly Politics,"

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:53.910
but our insight was that
we could make projections

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:55.940
in the great majority of House races

00:41:55.940 --> 00:41:57.850
knowing nothing about the current cycle,

00:41:57.850 --> 00:42:01.943
just based on whether the
incumbent was a decent incumbent,

00:42:03.318 --> 00:42:06.030
and which you can measure
in kind of some simple ways,

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:07.800
and the tilt of the districts,

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:11.350
and we've been doing this for years,

00:42:11.350 --> 00:42:16.180
we could project the 2020 House
races in about 85% of races

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:18.620
within a week of the 2018 elections

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:20.450
and essentially get all
but one or two right,

00:42:20.450 --> 00:42:23.800
in some cycles, we get
every single one right,

00:42:23.800 --> 00:42:25.690
so it means that whatever campaigning

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:28.120
happens in those districts doesn't matter.

00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:30.570
If you look at the underlying
nature of districts

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:33.560
and note the pattern here,

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:36.700
it isn't just happened
when districts are drawn,

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:40.680
it's happening between
cycles of districting,

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:43.380
that districts are becoming
less and less competitive,

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.420
and you're seeing voters
split their tickets less

00:42:48.410 --> 00:42:51.060
and they are voting more consistently,

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:54.920
so back when I helped
start FairVote back in '92,

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:56.960
at that time, in Congress,

00:42:56.960 --> 00:43:01.960
about 112 seats had members
who represented a district

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:04.610
won by the other major party's
presidential candidate,

00:43:04.610 --> 00:43:05.970
so more than a quarter of seats

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:08.600
were held by someone who
was of a different party

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:11.910
than the candidate that
carried that district

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:13.400
in the presidential race,

00:43:13.400 --> 00:43:15.820
we're now down to that's only 16 people,

00:43:15.820 --> 00:43:18.720
less than 4%, so almost total alignment,

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:21.187
the U.S. Senate is the same way,

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:25.270
state legislative races have

00:43:25.270 --> 00:43:27.890
become more and more lined
up with presidential races

00:43:27.890 --> 00:43:30.550
just based on the partisan
label drives behavior.

00:43:30.550 --> 00:43:31.920
At the same time,

00:43:31.920 --> 00:43:34.560
the country's going through
this Big Sort concept,

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:39.560
the idea that people are
becoming more alike politically

00:43:39.970 --> 00:43:41.580
with others where they live,

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:42.510
and you can measure that,

00:43:42.510 --> 00:43:44.290
again, this isn't gerrymandered,

00:43:44.290 --> 00:43:45.960
this is just sort of counties,

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:48.640
and counties have become
more and more aligned

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:50.900
with one party or the other

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:52.960
at the same time that people
are splitting their tickets

00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:53.873
less and less,

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:58.510
and you can look at
sort of the red-blue map

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:00.880
if you have our simplistic
system that we have

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:03.070
where it's just Democrats and Republicans,

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:04.710
and there's much more coherence

00:44:04.710 --> 00:44:07.340
and sort of solid blocks of color,

00:44:07.340 --> 00:44:11.033
which also plays out in
predictability of races.

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:16.700
So let me talk about the
two ideas that we focus on,

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:18.140
both of these are statutory,

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:19.980
both of these are getting
increasing attention,

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:21.890
ranked-choice voting's
getting a lot of use,

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:23.910
so that is the idea of replacing

00:44:23.910 --> 00:44:25.810
when you vote for one person,

00:44:25.810 --> 00:44:28.515
or you vote at all, essentially,

00:44:28.515 --> 00:44:30.670
you go for a ballot where you
can just vote for one person,

00:44:30.670 --> 00:44:31.690
a single-choice ballot,

00:44:31.690 --> 00:44:32.550
sort of all or nothing,

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:34.680
you either give a vote to someone or not,

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:35.610
with a ranked-choice ballot,

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:36.760
and I'll explain what that means,

00:44:36.760 --> 00:44:38.300
but it's something different,

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:40.100
and then you replace
the winner-take-all rule

00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:41.330
with a proportional system,

00:44:41.330 --> 00:44:43.130
kind of like what we heard about

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:45.270
was going on in Switzerland,

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:48.040
and indeed, is used in most
countries in some form.

00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:49.900
So a ranked-choice ballot,

00:44:49.900 --> 00:44:52.200
here's a very basic one,

00:44:52.200 --> 00:44:54.470
instead of just ticking off one candidate,

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:56.257
you get to say, "Hey, here's my candidate

00:44:56.257 --> 00:44:57.207
"who's my first choice,

00:44:57.207 --> 00:44:59.317
"but I actually do have something to say

00:44:59.317 --> 00:45:00.560
"about other candidates,"

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:03.090
in this case, the person's
saying Brown's our first choice,

00:45:03.090 --> 00:45:05.560
Castro's our second, Adams' our third,

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:09.370
this is simple and easy for voters to do,

00:45:09.370 --> 00:45:10.640
it was introduced, say,

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:14.930
in five states' democratic parties,

00:45:14.930 --> 00:45:16.710
used in presidential primaries last year,

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:18.850
first time, vote-by-mail ballots,

00:45:18.850 --> 00:45:21.447
99.9% of people cast a valid ballot

00:45:21.447 --> 00:45:24.113
and a great majority of
people ranked more than one.

00:45:25.190 --> 00:45:27.500
So here's what you do
when you count them up,

00:45:27.500 --> 00:45:29.450
so you add up the first choices only,

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:31.390
so you look at that ballot and
you only count first choices.

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:33.600
If someone wins more
than half of the vote,

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:35.590
you have a first-round winner, that's it.

00:45:35.590 --> 00:45:40.590
If not, the candidate that is
in last place is dropped out,

00:45:40.750 --> 00:45:42.660
and you're only counting
that ballot's candidates

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:43.910
for your next choice,

00:45:43.910 --> 00:45:46.760
so everyone has only one vote that counts,

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:49.990
but you get a backup if
your favorite is out,

00:45:49.990 --> 00:45:51.910
so in this case, it goes down to three,

00:45:51.910 --> 00:45:53.740
you still don't have a majority winner,

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:55.280
you knock out the last-place candidate,

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:58.380
and voila, you have somewhere
over half the votes,

00:45:58.380 --> 00:45:59.880
and that's all there is to it.

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:02.140
So what does this mean politically?

00:46:02.140 --> 00:46:05.100
So one is that it creates this space

00:46:05.100 --> 00:46:06.680
to have more than two chances

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:09.120
and not have this immediate
finger-pointing dynamic

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:10.610
where like, "Why is that person running?"

00:46:10.610 --> 00:46:12.030
and, you know, right now,

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:14.890
you're having kind of a
debate on the Republican side

00:46:14.890 --> 00:46:17.730
where some people love Donald
Trump, some people don't,

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:19.210
and there's sort of talk about whether

00:46:19.210 --> 00:46:21.530
there should be another party,

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:22.547
and there's a lot of them saying like,

00:46:22.547 --> 00:46:25.390
"Well, we can't do that
because we'll split the vote."

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:27.440
You know, Ralph Nader
running as a Green Party

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:30.390
or Ross Perot as an
Independent or what have you

00:46:30.390 --> 00:46:32.440
that they don't talk
about whether that person

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:33.830
has an idea that's different

00:46:33.830 --> 00:46:36.430
or a political philosophy
that should be represented,

00:46:36.430 --> 00:46:37.690
it's just the dynamics

00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:40.070
of you can't have more than
two people in our politics,

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:44.470
if you break that down, you
begin to break down the binary.

00:46:44.470 --> 00:46:47.270
What it means kind of
politically with outcomes

00:46:47.270 --> 00:46:49.070
is that candidates,

00:46:49.070 --> 00:46:51.620
and you really see this
particularly well in city elections,

00:46:51.620 --> 00:46:54.810
we have lots of good data
from cities and primaries,

00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:58.390
and it encourages candidates
to engage with more voters,

00:46:58.390 --> 00:46:59.760
run more positive campaigns,

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:01.720
not everyone being
lovely to everyone else,

00:47:01.720 --> 00:47:04.937
but it incentivizes reaching out,

00:47:04.937 --> 00:47:08.790
and the winners typically
get affirmative preferences

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:12.100
from 65, 70% of the voters,

00:47:12.100 --> 00:47:14.980
which is an indication of
that sort of reaching out,

00:47:14.980 --> 00:47:16.470
and again, it's being used quite a bit

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:17.730
in the United States now,

00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:21.100
we're up to two states using
it for president and Congress,

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:22.750
90-plus colleges and universities

00:47:22.750 --> 00:47:24.270
sort of do it on their own in 20 cities,

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:26.240
we might see as many as
10 more cities using it

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:28.080
in Utah this year.

00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:29.523
Let's now turn to multi-member districts,

00:47:29.523 --> 00:47:31.610
now, we see a lot of
multi-member districts

00:47:31.610 --> 00:47:33.727
in local elections in the United States,

00:47:33.727 --> 00:47:36.110
you still see a number of
state legislatures with them,

00:47:36.110 --> 00:47:38.760
I actually have three House
members here in Maryland,

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:42.410
we heard some about them in Switzerland,

00:47:42.410 --> 00:47:43.243
now, there's a lot of ways

00:47:43.243 --> 00:47:45.120
that you can use multi-member districts,

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:49.370
but the basic idea is you change
from having only one person

00:47:49.370 --> 00:47:51.640
trying to represent everyone
in a geographic area,

00:47:51.640 --> 00:47:54.060
so Congress or the U.S. House,

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:56.860
not a single U.S. House member represents

00:47:56.860 --> 00:47:59.100
any of the same people
that anyone else does,

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:02.380
and you go to having, say, in
this example in Massachusetts,

00:48:02.380 --> 00:48:05.500
you go to having bigger districts
with more representatives,

00:48:05.500 --> 00:48:07.930
so it's three, three,
and three in this case,

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:09.540
if we use the ranked-choice system,

00:48:09.540 --> 00:48:13.820
single transferable vote in
our classical terminology,

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:16.270
it only takes 50% to win,

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:18.330
it takes them a little
over a quarter of the vote

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:19.670
to win one out of three seats,

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:24.300
50% to win two, more than
75% to sweep all three,

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:26.640
that high percentage of what
it takes to win all three

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:28.373
has a really interesting impact.

00:48:29.750 --> 00:48:32.880
So what does it mean for
polarization and representation?

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:34.370
David Brooks wrote a great column

00:48:34.370 --> 00:48:36.559
in "The New York Times"
a couple years ago,

00:48:36.559 --> 00:48:38.140
(indistinct) "One Reform to Save America,"

00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:40.620
this is this Fair Representation Act,

00:48:40.620 --> 00:48:41.750
which has been introduced

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:43.650
and is about to be (indistinct) again,

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:47.150
and what it means for polarization

00:48:47.150 --> 00:48:50.090
is that, really, there's no
safe seats anymore, right?

00:48:50.090 --> 00:48:52.300
So all that kind of
blocked out of the country,

00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:54.120
that stark red and blue,

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:57.050
in fact, pretty much every single district

00:48:57.050 --> 00:48:59.070
is gonna have both
parties having the power

00:48:59.070 --> 00:49:01.400
to help elect someone from it,

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:04.570
you can have Republicans in
New York City and other cities,

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:06.250
Democrats in rural areas,

00:49:06.250 --> 00:49:07.540
and different kinds of Democrats

00:49:07.540 --> 00:49:09.390
and different kinds of Republicans,

00:49:09.390 --> 00:49:12.840
third parties and Independents
have a challenge to win,

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:14.740
but they can hold the
major parties accountable

00:49:14.740 --> 00:49:16.690
in a different kind of way,

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:17.790
and then you have a Congress

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:22.420
that has a much more centered
political center represented,

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:24.650
but also, it has sort of
vibrant representation

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:25.483
of left and right,

00:49:25.483 --> 00:49:27.560
and of course, we're not linear that way

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:30.810
and it's sort of a more
three-dimensional reality

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:33.620
that that would allow it
to function differently.

00:49:33.620 --> 00:49:36.910
So it was H.R. 4000 in the last Congress,

00:49:36.910 --> 00:49:40.380
sort of to sum up the
representation impacts,

00:49:40.380 --> 00:49:43.440
our simulations show that
with this system in place,

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:46.480
every single state that
has at least three seats,

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:50.410
you would have every voter
probably end up being represented

00:49:50.410 --> 00:49:54.680
by candidates from both major parties,

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:56.500
and again, those independents
and third parties

00:49:56.500 --> 00:49:57.850
having a fighting chance,

00:49:57.850 --> 00:50:00.267
women, we see a dramatic
increase in representation,

00:50:00.267 --> 00:50:01.880
there's a lot of good
data that just showing

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:03.220
going to multi-member districts

00:50:03.220 --> 00:50:05.640
would change that to the more fluid nature

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:08.220
of choice and competition

00:50:08.220 --> 00:50:11.600
would very likely lead
to a significant boost

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:13.920
just from the get-go from this system,

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:15.900
and people of color would have

00:50:15.900 --> 00:50:17.910
really dramatically
different opportunities

00:50:17.910 --> 00:50:20.720
to both participate and win,

00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:23.890
one way of showing that is that
you can go from California,

00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:26.840
a whole line of states all the
way to my state of Maryland,

00:50:26.840 --> 00:50:28.990
and every single one of those states,

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:33.140
every person of color
would have the opportunity

00:50:33.140 --> 00:50:35.320
to join with other like-minded voters

00:50:35.320 --> 00:50:37.200
to help elect a candidate of choice,

00:50:37.200 --> 00:50:38.300
which would be quite dramatic

00:50:38.300 --> 00:50:39.640
when you think that that's the South

00:50:39.640 --> 00:50:40.880
and that's where part of the regions

00:50:40.880 --> 00:50:42.930
that have been so racially polarized.

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:44.360
So I look forward to chatting
about it with people,

00:50:44.360 --> 00:50:45.193
I'm Rob Richie,

00:50:45.193 --> 00:50:49.173
and I'll pass it on to my next person.

00:50:51.680 --> 00:50:53.193
- Okay, thanks very much.

00:50:54.410 --> 00:50:56.410
So these have been great,

00:50:56.410 --> 00:51:00.450
one thing, I wanted to
remind people to please use

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:02.900
the Raise Hand button on the
bottom of the Zoom screen

00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:04.200
to ask your question,

00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:05.880
or else the chat function,

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:09.873
I'm gonna collect those
and then ask questions,

00:51:11.060 --> 00:51:12.280
but to get us started,

00:51:12.280 --> 00:51:14.260
I think there's one question

00:51:14.260 --> 00:51:16.070
I'd like to ask all the panelists,

00:51:16.070 --> 00:51:20.650
which is, how much do you hope

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:23.530
that these different electoral systems

00:51:23.530 --> 00:51:27.880
will make a difference in
terms of overall outcome?

00:51:27.880 --> 00:51:31.670
So if we look around the
world, we see big differences,

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:33.830
certainly across different
electoral systems,

00:51:33.830 --> 00:51:35.140
but I'm struck by the fact

00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:38.960
that even within places that
have the same electoral system,

00:51:38.960 --> 00:51:41.000
there's just enormous variation

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:42.970
in terms of some of the negative things

00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:44.857
that people have said that they wanted

00:51:44.857 --> 00:51:47.020
via a fix in democracy,

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:48.900
but that tells us that electoral rules

00:51:48.900 --> 00:51:50.600
can make a difference,

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:52.860
but not all the difference,

00:51:52.860 --> 00:51:55.900
and I wanted to get some
sense from the panelists

00:51:55.900 --> 00:51:58.233
of how much of a difference they think

00:51:58.233 --> 00:52:02.390
that changing to these
incredibly interesting systems

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:04.910
will make to outcomes they care about,

00:52:04.910 --> 00:52:07.200
and then secondly, whether
they think there's any cost

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:12.200
to the particular mechanisms
that they're talking about,

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:15.700
is there a trade-off, and
how do they see the downside?

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:17.750
So we can go in the order

00:52:20.258 --> 00:52:23.020
in which we started,

00:52:23.020 --> 00:52:24.300
and then while that's happening,

00:52:24.300 --> 00:52:26.680
I'll look at people's comments in the chat

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:28.940
and we'll get ready to
call on people in the chat,

00:52:28.940 --> 00:52:30.590
and when I do call on you,

00:52:30.590 --> 00:52:32.190
we're going to ask you to go live

00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:36.223
and ask the question yourself on video,

00:52:38.525 --> 00:52:40.080
and so please be ready for that.

00:52:40.080 --> 00:52:41.843
Okay, Elisa, first to you.

00:52:42.900 --> 00:52:44.530
- Yes, thanks so much, Steven,

00:52:44.530 --> 00:52:46.920
and thanks again to all the panelists

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:48.520
and everyone for being here.

00:52:48.520 --> 00:52:50.820
Yeah, that's an incredibly
interesting question

00:52:50.820 --> 00:52:53.540
and I think it's very hard for me

00:52:53.540 --> 00:52:57.480
to say exactly how much
change it can affect.

00:52:57.480 --> 00:53:00.480
I do think it's, of course,

00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:03.880
who is setting the rules

00:53:03.880 --> 00:53:06.620
and setting the tone for our country

00:53:06.620 --> 00:53:08.310
is incredibly important,

00:53:08.310 --> 00:53:11.483
so I do think that it,

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:16.210
changing how we elect people,
changing if we elect people,

00:53:16.210 --> 00:53:19.320
all of these things can
have incredible impact.

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:21.180
Now, quantifying that in some way,

00:53:21.180 --> 00:53:23.790
I'm not quite sure how to do that,

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:26.830
and while I do think there is a cost,

00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:30.890
I think that cost is just
in terms of time and effort

00:53:30.890 --> 00:53:33.980
and more participation,

00:53:33.980 --> 00:53:35.610
which sounds like a cost, right?

00:53:35.610 --> 00:53:37.270
There's more work that has to be done,

00:53:37.270 --> 00:53:40.730
but that naturally is more
work of involving more people,

00:53:40.730 --> 00:53:42.530
involving everyone into the whole process

00:53:42.530 --> 00:53:45.360
that is affecting their lives
and is affecting our future,

00:53:45.360 --> 00:53:47.840
so it's a good cost to pay.

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:50.470
If the cost is more people
have to participate,

00:53:50.470 --> 00:53:52.633
then that's exactly what we wanna do.

00:53:52.633 --> 00:53:54.005
Thank you.

00:53:54.005 --> 00:53:54.838
- Mark?

00:53:58.260 --> 00:54:02.520
- I mean, obviously, the system
is large and complicated,

00:54:02.520 --> 00:54:07.520
and no one change is enough in any way,

00:54:08.280 --> 00:54:10.940
a large part of what I
have been thinking about

00:54:10.940 --> 00:54:15.490
has to do with just the
incentives of elected officials

00:54:15.490 --> 00:54:18.510
because most people

00:54:18.510 --> 00:54:21.350
tend to respond to their incentives,

00:54:21.350 --> 00:54:23.270
and if something helps them personally,

00:54:23.270 --> 00:54:26.060
they're inclined to
move in that direction,

00:54:26.060 --> 00:54:29.600
and so I've been personally focused

00:54:29.600 --> 00:54:32.230
on trying to think about how

00:54:32.230 --> 00:54:35.400
to mitigate bad incentives in the system

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:37.060
and create good incentives,

00:54:37.060 --> 00:54:40.970
but there is obviously so many pieces

00:54:40.970 --> 00:54:45.970
to how the government
is structured and runs,

00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:50.490
and I think that we're
really at a point where,

00:54:50.490 --> 00:54:51.470
I mean, essentially,

00:54:51.470 --> 00:54:55.620
the systems that we're
running with today in the U.S.

00:54:55.620 --> 00:54:58.910
are 100 or 200 or more years old,

00:54:58.910 --> 00:55:01.590
and there have been so many changes

00:55:01.590 --> 00:55:04.410
to society, to scale, to technology,

00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:05.860
I actually think there are,

00:55:05.860 --> 00:55:10.350
you know, the possibility
of openness and transparency

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:12.900
due to new computer technology

00:55:12.900 --> 00:55:15.050
means that it's possible for people

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:18.110
to have much more insight into the details

00:55:18.110 --> 00:55:21.530
of how government works and
to perhaps micro-target it,

00:55:21.530 --> 00:55:23.230
so I think there's a whole,

00:55:23.230 --> 00:55:25.230
I mean, I'm hoping in the next generation

00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:29.790
we'll see a range of new ideas
and a whole range of changes

00:55:29.790 --> 00:55:33.340
to improve the efficiency
and honesty of the system

00:55:33.340 --> 00:55:36.380
because right now, government doesn't,

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:37.640
you know, it kind of clunks along,

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:41.763
but it is by no means
working well in most places.

00:55:42.850 --> 00:55:44.100
- [Steve] Thanks. Helene?

00:55:51.200 --> 00:55:53.920
- So I think any of these proposals

00:55:53.920 --> 00:55:55.620
are improvement on the status quo.

00:55:56.740 --> 00:55:59.100
I do feel, though, that it will create

00:56:00.710 --> 00:56:02.420
more visible diversity,

00:56:02.420 --> 00:56:04.150
but without much of a,

00:56:04.150 --> 00:56:06.680
or not enough of a committee
difference, if you will,

00:56:06.680 --> 00:56:08.320
in the sense that at the end of the day,

00:56:08.320 --> 00:56:11.430
elections remain, as I insisted, elitist,

00:56:11.430 --> 00:56:14.640
so you will bring about

00:56:14.640 --> 00:56:17.577
more people of color and women,

00:56:17.577 --> 00:56:20.590
the sort of, like, demographic
categories we care about

00:56:20.590 --> 00:56:24.380
because they're easy to
understand and grapple with,

00:56:24.380 --> 00:56:26.480
but there are other
categories we underestimate,

00:56:26.480 --> 00:56:29.380
like certain types of minds
that correspond to personalities

00:56:29.380 --> 00:56:30.520
that don't sell well in election,

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:33.250
and so I worry that we're still

00:56:33.250 --> 00:56:37.200
gonna end up with very biased
groups of people in power,

00:56:37.200 --> 00:56:40.170
and I think we are losing out on the power

00:56:41.640 --> 00:56:43.530
of greater diversity that way

00:56:43.530 --> 00:56:46.080
that would also be a
lot more, again, tired,

00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:47.570
so that's my worry,

00:56:47.570 --> 00:56:49.080
and then, I would say that,

00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:50.960
this is perhaps a question to Mark,

00:56:50.960 --> 00:56:53.443
I worry about the transparency

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:58.210
of this scheme, which
is quite complicated,

00:56:58.210 --> 00:57:02.370
do we not lose some democratic appeal

00:57:02.370 --> 00:57:03.203
when we have these multiple steps

00:57:03.203 --> 00:57:06.870
and there's some elections,
some random selection,

00:57:06.870 --> 00:57:07.790
don't people lose

00:57:09.091 --> 00:57:12.260
the chain of accountability

00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:13.373
in those schemes?

00:57:15.680 --> 00:57:17.080
- Yeah, Mark, please answer.

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:22.190
- I actually think that this can be,

00:57:22.190 --> 00:57:25.100
you can still have quite
a bit of accountability

00:57:25.100 --> 00:57:27.220
because, I mean, right now,

00:57:27.220 --> 00:57:31.410
I mean, we elect all sorts of
horrible elected officials,

00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:34.630
and I don't think you
hardly ever see anyone

00:57:34.630 --> 00:57:38.803
who feels particularly bad
because they cast a vote,

00:57:38.803 --> 00:57:42.820
I mean, no one really feels
any ownership of any decision,

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:46.150
I mean, so certainly the
process that I had suggested

00:57:46.150 --> 00:57:49.130
I intend it to be highly transparent,

00:57:49.130 --> 00:57:50.900
so people, you know, we can see

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:54.930
how the deliberations
work, what the output is,

00:57:54.930 --> 00:57:56.520
but also, then, people,

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:58.770
you know, a small number of people

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:02.240
would be very much responsible

00:58:02.240 --> 00:58:04.450
for particular leaders,

00:58:04.450 --> 00:58:07.570
so if that person a did a bad job,

00:58:07.570 --> 00:58:10.137
you could go back and
say, "You group of people,

00:58:10.137 --> 00:58:13.370
"you chose them, it's sort of your fault."

00:58:13.370 --> 00:58:16.560
Again, nothing is perfect,
everything is imperfect,

00:58:16.560 --> 00:58:20.970
but I do think that questioning,

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:23.670
and again, Helene, you're
doing that with your own work,

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:27.530
the idea that just somehow
polling large numbers of people

00:58:27.530 --> 00:58:29.680
leads to a good decision,

00:58:29.680 --> 00:58:32.240
I think that we see
the problems with that,

00:58:32.240 --> 00:58:35.020
and so trying to find an alternative,

00:58:35.020 --> 00:58:37.230
I think any of these solutions,

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:38.890
you have to try in reality

00:58:38.890 --> 00:58:40.990
and then sort of see
what works in iterates,

00:58:40.990 --> 00:58:45.990
I don't think anything is
perfect in the abstract

00:58:46.030 --> 00:58:47.353
or in all contexts.

00:58:48.350 --> 00:58:49.453
- Thank you. Rob?

00:58:52.730 --> 00:58:53.563
- Well, I'll say one thing,

00:58:53.563 --> 00:58:57.750
I guess I would be loath at this point

00:58:57.750 --> 00:59:01.600
to not ground any system

00:59:01.600 --> 00:59:04.180
in a one-person-one-vote electoral system,

00:59:04.180 --> 00:59:07.857
so I am a big believer
that that's important,

00:59:07.857 --> 00:59:09.300
and that's a way of sort of measuring

00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:12.270
consent of the governed,
having this conversation,

00:59:12.270 --> 00:59:16.900
I think there's interesting
ways to add in some ideas

00:59:18.480 --> 00:59:22.270
that would add dimensions without,

00:59:22.270 --> 00:59:25.340
but also, let's start by
making that dimension work

00:59:25.340 --> 00:59:27.910
because we have so far to
go in the United States

00:59:27.910 --> 00:59:29.320
that it's sort of easy to start there

00:59:29.320 --> 00:59:31.900
because it is so problematic

00:59:31.900 --> 00:59:35.200
and so, in some ways,
transparently straightforward

00:59:35.200 --> 00:59:37.800
how to make it much, much better,

00:59:37.800 --> 00:59:40.250
our slogan at FairVote
is A More Perfect Union,

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:42.400
and I think part of that choice

00:59:42.400 --> 00:59:45.450
was the belief that
it's an ongoing process,

00:59:45.450 --> 00:59:46.730
we're not gonna get perfection,

00:59:46.730 --> 00:59:49.910
so no, it's not like we
get ranked-choice voting

00:59:49.910 --> 00:59:52.430
for all of our elections and
the Fair Representation Act,

00:59:52.430 --> 00:59:56.100
and we can just, you know, say we're done,

00:59:56.100 --> 00:59:59.530
but it would be starkly
and importantly better,

00:59:59.530 --> 01:00:01.890
and I think one area

01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:05.540
that we can think about
also is sort of embracing

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:06.520
the fact that we have

01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:08.700
a particularly federalized
system in the United States,

01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:10.800
so we have about half a
million elected offices,

01:00:10.800 --> 01:00:12.160
we have a lot of local government,

01:00:12.160 --> 01:00:15.193
more than I think any other country,

01:00:16.660 --> 01:00:18.180
or at least most countries,

01:00:18.180 --> 01:00:20.400
and that there's an opportunity in there

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:21.920
to try to see if there's ways

01:00:21.920 --> 01:00:24.930
that we can generate more citizen
participation and dialogue

01:00:25.910 --> 01:00:26.860
that, in a sense,

01:00:26.860 --> 01:00:29.180
captures some of what people
are talking about here,

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:30.410
but the reality is right now

01:00:30.410 --> 01:00:32.090
people are actually less interested

01:00:32.090 --> 01:00:34.040
in participating at that level

01:00:34.040 --> 01:00:35.330
even though it's closer to home,

01:00:35.330 --> 01:00:37.180
so it's something that I
think that we should work on.

01:00:37.180 --> 01:00:38.200
Last thing I'll say,

01:00:38.200 --> 01:00:40.140
big fan of things like
participatory budgeting,

01:00:40.140 --> 01:00:41.840
there already are a lot
of kind of creative things

01:00:41.840 --> 01:00:42.673
going on out there

01:00:42.673 --> 01:00:45.940
and we just need to keep
thinking about different models

01:00:45.940 --> 01:00:48.630
to add on to the electoral one.

01:00:48.630 --> 01:00:49.880
- Yeah, Helene?

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:53.280
- Yeah, I just wanna
reply to the point that,

01:00:55.640 --> 01:00:57.290
if we care about one-person-one-vote,

01:00:57.290 --> 01:00:59.210
we have to stay with elections,

01:00:59.210 --> 01:01:02.200
I don't think so, we can
have one-person-one-vote

01:01:02.200 --> 01:01:04.500
used in the context of referenda

01:01:04.500 --> 01:01:08.430
and, you know, all kinds
of mass popular voting,

01:01:08.430 --> 01:01:11.210
what I object to is not the
one-person-one-vote principle,

01:01:11.210 --> 01:01:12.210
which is, of course, essential

01:01:12.210 --> 01:01:14.660
and would be a part of an open democracy,

01:01:14.660 --> 01:01:17.320
but the use of that just to select people

01:01:17.320 --> 01:01:19.390
who then exercise power on our behalf,

01:01:19.390 --> 01:01:24.050
I think we should be using
this one-person-one-vote right

01:01:24.050 --> 01:01:27.360
to make decisions ourselves
based on the recommendations

01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:30.323
of a randomly selected body or directly.

01:01:32.810 --> 01:01:34.300
It's not a real choice.

01:01:36.797 --> 01:01:40.180
- And one thing I think makes
a big difference in the U.S.

01:01:40.180 --> 01:01:44.430
is just the closed primaries
in so many different states

01:01:44.430 --> 01:01:45.480
that influence who gets there,

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:47.850
even in places with the
same electoral system,

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:50.010
you see that having a huge difference.

01:01:50.010 --> 01:01:54.500
Anyway, let's open it
up now to the audience,

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:57.710
there is a huge wealth of
knowledge and experience

01:01:59.180 --> 01:02:03.090
and cases that they know about
in terms of the audience,

01:02:03.090 --> 01:02:05.707
so first we'll go to Caity Welz, Caity?

01:02:09.540 --> 01:02:12.650
- [Caity] Hi, my name's Caity Welz,

01:02:12.650 --> 01:02:16.560
I'm a government teacher in
Springfield Massachusetts.

01:02:16.560 --> 01:02:17.610
I have a couple questions,

01:02:17.610 --> 01:02:19.460
but I'm gonna start with one for Rob

01:02:19.460 --> 01:02:22.403
just in case I only get the one, (laughs)

01:02:23.454 --> 01:02:24.500
I think one of the fears

01:02:24.500 --> 01:02:29.070
in switching up the way that
we vote is that safe states,

01:02:29.070 --> 01:02:30.880
whether they are red or blue,

01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:33.960
by taking steps to make
their elections more fair

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:37.350
could give an advantage to
what's seen as the other side

01:02:37.350 --> 01:02:40.900
by taking the safety
out of their elections,

01:02:40.900 --> 01:02:42.970
while states that don't adopt the measures

01:02:42.970 --> 01:02:44.450
retain those safe seats,

01:02:44.450 --> 01:02:46.430
so there's two parts to my question,

01:02:46.430 --> 01:02:48.170
do you think there's a way to avoid

01:02:48.170 --> 01:02:50.150
this unintended consequence?

01:02:50.150 --> 01:02:54.980
And if the change was to
be implemented nationwide,

01:02:54.980 --> 01:02:56.130
do you think there's a way to do it

01:02:56.130 --> 01:03:00.490
within the confines of the
Constitution as it exists now

01:03:00.490 --> 01:03:03.130
given that states may
throw in electoral rules

01:03:03.130 --> 01:03:05.470
since it seems unlikely that
we would see an amendment

01:03:05.470 --> 01:03:07.720
to increase voter fairness

01:03:07.720 --> 01:03:11.110
given the lack of incentive
for existing congresspersons

01:03:11.110 --> 01:03:12.990
to jeopardize their positions

01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:15.433
by removing the safety of their seats?

01:03:17.390 --> 01:03:19.910
- Well, that's a good
question, and I'll say,

01:03:19.910 --> 01:03:22.010
I saw as part of your chat question too

01:03:22.010 --> 01:03:23.060
about ranked-choice voting

01:03:23.060 --> 01:03:25.020
having lost in the
ballot in Massachusetts,

01:03:25.020 --> 01:03:27.770
the good news was that's the only time

01:03:27.770 --> 01:03:30.360
it's lost in the ballot
in the last five years

01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:31.270
and it's been on the ballot

01:03:31.270 --> 01:03:33.640
in more than 15 other jurisdictions,

01:03:33.640 --> 01:03:36.610
then including cities,
it's won by an average

01:03:36.610 --> 01:03:38.030
of more than two to one in every city

01:03:38.030 --> 01:03:39.530
voting on it for a while,

01:03:39.530 --> 01:03:40.560
so it doesn't mean you can

01:03:40.560 --> 01:03:41.950
throw it on the ballot everywhere and win,

01:03:41.950 --> 01:03:45.130
but there's a lot of sort of
I think interest for change,

01:03:45.130 --> 01:03:48.690
and the more we use it, I
think the better it can look.

01:03:48.690 --> 01:03:51.920
For Congress, you know, you're
right about the challenge of,

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:54.560
how do you have one state be fair and not,

01:03:54.560 --> 01:03:55.460
you see it play out

01:03:55.460 --> 01:03:57.230
when people talk about
redistricting reform,

01:03:57.230 --> 01:03:58.987
and, you know, like, "Well, if we have

01:03:58.987 --> 01:04:01.680
"kind of a better redistricting process,

01:04:01.680 --> 01:04:02.513
"a fair one,

01:04:02.513 --> 01:04:03.787
"that might mean more competition

01:04:03.787 --> 01:04:07.090
"and turnover of incumbents in our state,"

01:04:07.090 --> 01:04:08.160
and the other states don't,

01:04:08.160 --> 01:04:10.540
then you know, nationally,
that's actually not fair,

01:04:10.540 --> 01:04:12.060
even if it's fair in the state,

01:04:12.060 --> 01:04:15.570
but a lot of states have
moved ahead anyway on that,

01:04:15.570 --> 01:04:19.100
so that isn't a barrier to
change happening state by state.

01:04:19.100 --> 01:04:22.370
There is a requirement for House
elections that's statutory,

01:04:22.370 --> 01:04:24.320
that's important, it's a federal statute,

01:04:24.320 --> 01:04:26.220
most recently passed in 1967

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:28.380
after not having been
in place for a while,

01:04:28.380 --> 01:04:30.490
which is to require
single-member districts,

01:04:30.490 --> 01:04:31.740
so this is a,

01:04:31.740 --> 01:04:35.170
the early congresses had a
lot of multi-member districts

01:04:35.170 --> 01:04:38.330
at large elections for Congress,

01:04:38.330 --> 01:04:42.070
and when a 1929 law that
was sort of associated

01:04:42.070 --> 01:04:44.360
with also capping House size,

01:04:44.360 --> 01:04:46.800
kind of ridiculously at 435

01:04:46.800 --> 01:04:48.550
rather than having an ongoing conversation

01:04:48.550 --> 01:04:50.490
about increasing the House,

01:04:50.490 --> 01:04:52.167
it opened the door to
multi-member districts,

01:04:52.167 --> 01:04:53.890
and some states were
doing that in the '60s,

01:04:53.890 --> 01:04:54.887
so anyway, Congress said,

01:04:54.887 --> 01:04:57.707
"Well, let's have everyone
be quote-unquote fair

01:04:57.707 --> 01:04:59.570
"by having single-member districts,"

01:04:59.570 --> 01:05:01.490
that isn't actually super fair,

01:05:01.490 --> 01:05:03.040
as we demonstrated,

01:05:03.040 --> 01:05:07.230
and mostly noncompetitive races

01:05:07.230 --> 01:05:10.780
and a lot of gerrymandering
going on in a bunch of states,

01:05:10.780 --> 01:05:14.390
and so the theoretical basis
of the Fair Representation Act,

01:05:14.390 --> 01:05:15.430
which is statutory,

01:05:15.430 --> 01:05:17.540
doesn't require changing the Constitution,

01:05:17.540 --> 01:05:20.850
is to say, well, let's actually
have states be truly fair

01:05:20.850 --> 01:05:24.830
and all simultaneously put
in multi-member districts

01:05:24.830 --> 01:05:26.000
with a ranked-choice system

01:05:26.000 --> 01:05:28.330
and have those benefits
that I talked about.

01:05:28.330 --> 01:05:33.330
So the politics of that are big and bold,

01:05:33.450 --> 01:05:34.900
I think, grounded in the logic,

01:05:34.900 --> 01:05:37.180
that that's a good way to do it,

01:05:37.180 --> 01:05:38.170
but there's other approaches

01:05:38.170 --> 01:05:39.580
that may end up being the way it goes,

01:05:39.580 --> 01:05:40.990
which is sort of allowing states,

01:05:40.990 --> 01:05:42.500
again, to have the option to do it,

01:05:42.500 --> 01:05:44.210
and it goes more sort of state by state,

01:05:44.210 --> 01:05:46.090
but we're sort of
launching the conversation,

01:05:46.090 --> 01:05:47.830
and we're doing it in a climate

01:05:47.830 --> 01:05:50.010
where we really need to do something,

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:51.960
and that's, I think, the
impetus for Congress,

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:54.420
they're kind of stuck in a situation

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:57.760
that a lot of them feel
very challenged by,

01:05:57.760 --> 01:06:00.273
and I think that even if
their own seats are safe,

01:06:01.420 --> 01:06:04.161
operating in Congress to
get things done is not safe,

01:06:04.161 --> 01:06:06.010
or it's sort of hard for them to do

01:06:06.010 --> 01:06:08.750
what they actually wanna
do a lot of the times,

01:06:08.750 --> 01:06:10.500
and that creates, I think, the opportunity

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:12.590
to actually get the big changes

01:06:12.590 --> 01:06:14.593
that are usually really hard to win.

01:06:16.520 --> 01:06:17.463
- Okay, Caity, you get one more

01:06:17.463 --> 01:06:19.030
because you have questions

01:06:19.030 --> 01:06:21.760
for a number of the other
people, so pick one.

01:06:21.760 --> 01:06:23.320
- [Caity] Okay, oh, thank you so much

01:06:23.320 --> 01:06:24.940
for letting me ask another one.

01:06:24.940 --> 01:06:29.740
For Helene, what other
criticisms of jury selection

01:06:29.740 --> 01:06:33.170
in some areas of the United
States, including Connecticut,

01:06:33.170 --> 01:06:36.250
has highlighted the
potential bias in selection

01:06:36.250 --> 01:06:40.520
based on the data that they're
generating their lots from,

01:06:40.520 --> 01:06:42.050
could you comment on that?

01:06:42.050 --> 01:06:43.610
And do you have any suggestions

01:06:43.610 --> 01:06:47.303
about how that could be
avoided in your proposal?

01:06:49.332 --> 01:06:51.810
- So that's a fair criticism,

01:06:51.810 --> 01:06:53.300
I'll tell you what the French did,

01:06:53.300 --> 01:06:57.060
which sounds like, it seemed
to me, like a decent solution,

01:06:57.060 --> 01:06:59.170
they had to select 115 citizens,

01:06:59.170 --> 01:07:00.900
so what they did is that they generated

01:07:00.900 --> 01:07:02.300
300,000

01:07:06.040 --> 01:07:07.800
randomly generated phone numbers,

01:07:07.800 --> 01:07:09.410
it was a private company that did that,

01:07:09.410 --> 01:07:11.160
and then they called the people

01:07:11.160 --> 01:07:14.070
and gathered their demographics
and asked questions,

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:15.850
and then they applied
court assembly to that,

01:07:15.850 --> 01:07:17.210
and then in the end, they,

01:07:17.210 --> 01:07:22.210
so the criteria were age,
gender, education level,

01:07:22.440 --> 01:07:26.120
settlement, meaning whether
they were urban or rural,

01:07:26.120 --> 01:07:27.870
geographic origin, we didn't have race

01:07:27.870 --> 01:07:31.132
because it's not a legal
category in France,

01:07:31.132 --> 01:07:33.130
and one thing they failed to
take into account I think,

01:07:33.130 --> 01:07:34.590
which was, I think, a mistake,

01:07:34.590 --> 01:07:36.550
is their opinion about climate change,

01:07:36.550 --> 01:07:38.930
I think that would have been
important to sample as well,

01:07:38.930 --> 01:07:42.340
but at the end, they got
190 voluntary citizens

01:07:43.520 --> 01:07:46.500
that were, you know, not representative,

01:07:46.500 --> 01:07:47.830
I think it's too few anyway

01:07:47.830 --> 01:07:50.810
to represent statistically
the people of France,

01:07:50.810 --> 01:07:53.800
but they are really diverse

01:07:53.800 --> 01:07:57.263
and tracking the main categories
that we would care about,

01:07:58.760 --> 01:08:02.670
and that way, they had 150
citizens at each session,

01:08:02.670 --> 01:08:05.693
that (indistinct) it seemed
to be a decent method.

01:08:08.046 --> 01:08:11.600
- Okay, let's call on George Linzer,

01:08:11.600 --> 01:08:13.843
I hope that Christine
comes back in a moment.

01:08:16.340 --> 01:08:18.530
- [George] Hi, thanks, Steven.

01:08:18.530 --> 01:08:22.350
I am a publisher and editor
of the "American Leader,"

01:08:22.350 --> 01:08:27.350
and my question is related
to Steven's opening question,

01:08:28.493 --> 01:08:30.940
and first, I just wanna say

01:08:30.940 --> 01:08:34.080
I think systems are, no
matter what the system,

01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:37.010
it's, in a way, destined to be hacked,

01:08:37.010 --> 01:08:40.893
and we've seen that with the
current system that we have,

01:08:41.800 --> 01:08:43.280
and I do agree with Rob

01:08:44.158 --> 01:08:47.870
that making a more
perfect union is the goal

01:08:47.870 --> 01:08:49.473
and an ongoing process,

01:08:51.340 --> 01:08:55.420
so with that in mind, my question is this,

01:08:55.420 --> 01:08:58.610
where do you see the
role of civics education,

01:08:58.610 --> 01:09:01.160
and by that, I'm including
mandatory service

01:09:01.160 --> 01:09:03.333
and a nationally standardized curriculum,

01:09:04.820 --> 01:09:07.560
as a solution to the problems we face

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:10.260
relative to systemic change?

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:13.440
And I'll let anyone who
has a thought on that,

01:09:13.440 --> 01:09:14.640
I'd be happy to hear it.

01:09:16.720 --> 01:09:21.720
- Okay, just put your hand up
if you have thoughts on this.

01:09:21.739 --> 01:09:23.483
Helene, could you?

01:09:26.320 --> 01:09:29.340
- Well, I tend to think
that a lottocratic system

01:09:29.340 --> 01:09:31.300
has built-in civics education,

01:09:31.300 --> 01:09:33.720
which is not just listening
to boring lectures,

01:09:33.720 --> 01:09:36.290
it's actually doing and practicing

01:09:36.290 --> 01:09:38.530
what we are constantly preached, you know?

01:09:38.530 --> 01:09:41.533
So it's Socratic practice of deliberation,

01:09:42.560 --> 01:09:44.880
and you can start in school,

01:09:44.880 --> 01:09:47.330
you could start at the university,

01:09:47.330 --> 01:09:49.920
you could start in your company,

01:09:49.920 --> 01:09:51.780
you know, we've got all kinds of ways

01:09:51.780 --> 01:09:53.060
where we could reform governance

01:09:53.060 --> 01:09:55.190
so that people are randomly drawn

01:09:55.190 --> 01:09:57.320
into these decision-making bodies

01:09:57.320 --> 01:09:59.860
and learn about deliberating
with other people,

01:09:59.860 --> 01:10:02.100
dissent, people who disagree with them,

01:10:02.100 --> 01:10:03.700
and that forges,

01:10:03.700 --> 01:10:05.360
also just bring into contact

01:10:05.360 --> 01:10:07.260
people who would never meet otherwise,

01:10:08.741 --> 01:10:13.103
you know, elected circles
tend to be very homogenous

01:10:13.103 --> 01:10:14.756
on a number of dimensions,

01:10:14.756 --> 01:10:15.800
when you have a random sample

01:10:15.800 --> 01:10:18.507
and you bring together a farmer

01:10:18.507 --> 01:10:22.030
from, you know, I don't know, Wisconsin,

01:10:22.030 --> 01:10:24.873
and a designer from New York,

01:10:27.624 --> 01:10:30.180
and a nurse from Minnesota, I don't know,

01:10:30.180 --> 01:10:32.440
these are people who've
never really encountered

01:10:32.440 --> 01:10:33.940
people like themselves,

01:10:33.940 --> 01:10:35.340
so I think it's really important

01:10:35.340 --> 01:10:36.280
forging a sealing bond

01:10:36.280 --> 01:10:38.050
and, you know, bridging the gap

01:10:38.050 --> 01:10:41.403
between a Trump voter and
a Biden voter, crucially.

01:10:42.730 --> 01:10:45.473
- Just to add, just a
quick example of policy,

01:10:46.380 --> 01:10:48.510
it might be other Scandinavian countries,

01:10:48.510 --> 01:10:49.733
but definitely Norway,

01:10:50.640 --> 01:10:53.610
they sort of take this
responsibility really seriously

01:10:53.610 --> 01:10:56.210
that young people are
soon gonna be voters,

01:10:56.210 --> 01:10:57.700
gonna be participants,

01:10:57.700 --> 01:11:01.450
and there's much more
intensive civic education

01:11:01.450 --> 01:11:02.830
than is usual in the United States,

01:11:02.830 --> 01:11:05.470
but includes everyone going
to the national capitol

01:11:05.470 --> 01:11:07.810
for doing what often our elite students

01:11:07.810 --> 01:11:08.930
get to do here in the U.S.

01:11:08.930 --> 01:11:10.680
where there's sort of a mock congress

01:11:10.680 --> 01:11:13.587
and they get to see what Congress is like,

01:11:13.587 --> 01:11:14.600
but they all get to do that

01:11:14.600 --> 01:11:16.070
and they get to see the actual Congress

01:11:16.070 --> 01:11:19.930
and, you know, get to kind
of a more hands-on feel

01:11:19.930 --> 01:11:21.810
for what they soon are gonna be,

01:11:21.810 --> 01:11:23.950
and certainly, I think
we need to be investing

01:11:23.950 --> 01:11:26.063
and looking in those kinds of strategies.

01:11:27.280 --> 01:11:28.113
- Thank you.

01:11:29.430 --> 01:11:32.170
So I don't know if
Christine's mic is back on,

01:11:32.170 --> 01:11:34.290
but I'll ask her question,

01:11:34.290 --> 01:11:36.103
she said, "I like Mark Gorton's idea

01:11:36.103 --> 01:11:38.397
"and would support this
implementation for elections

01:11:38.397 --> 01:11:39.597
"such as president,

01:11:39.597 --> 01:11:41.277
"but it would seem an unlikely scenario

01:11:41.277 --> 01:11:42.317
"for smaller communities

01:11:42.317 --> 01:11:44.427
"for offices such as mayor, et cetera,

01:11:44.427 --> 01:11:45.967
"for you to include in a number of groups

01:11:45.967 --> 01:11:48.687
"and the people within each
group to do capable research,

01:11:48.687 --> 01:11:51.367
"and if representatives are
not held accountable now,

01:11:51.367 --> 01:11:52.707
"what would need to be put in place

01:11:52.707 --> 01:11:55.147
"for greater transparency
and less corruption

01:11:55.147 --> 01:11:58.220
"in order for a smooth transition?"

01:11:58.220 --> 01:12:00.330
So this gets us back to
this theme that we've got

01:12:00.330 --> 01:12:02.250
of, maybe there are other things you need

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:03.733
in addition to the rules,

01:12:04.850 --> 01:12:06.650
I'm curious how you'd respond, Mark.

01:12:08.420 --> 01:12:12.090
- I mean, I certainly
agree that the process

01:12:12.090 --> 01:12:14.840
that I outlined in my
slide was very heavy,

01:12:14.840 --> 01:12:18.510
I do think the idea of
a working-group model

01:12:18.510 --> 01:12:22.490
to select the mayor of
small towns still works,

01:12:22.490 --> 01:12:25.520
but maybe you're taking,

01:12:25.520 --> 01:12:27.870
you know, depending on
the size of the town,

01:12:27.870 --> 01:12:30.170
maybe it's one working
group with eight people

01:12:30.170 --> 01:12:32.960
who get together to choose someone

01:12:32.960 --> 01:12:34.760
that's probably known to them

01:12:34.760 --> 01:12:36.470
to be the mayor or something like that,

01:12:36.470 --> 01:12:41.280
I think you really can have
a smaller-scale process,

01:12:41.280 --> 01:12:42.400
and I really do think

01:12:42.400 --> 01:12:46.750
that there is value to
the deliberative process,

01:12:46.750 --> 01:12:50.490
and so this...

01:12:54.804 --> 01:12:56.770
you know, when people talk
and challenge each other,

01:12:56.770 --> 01:13:01.463
you will get a better output there.

01:13:02.435 --> 01:13:04.180
I'm sorry, what was the
second part of the question?

01:13:04.180 --> 01:13:07.230
Now I'm already, it was...

01:13:09.010 --> 01:13:10.163
- Let me see.

01:13:14.160 --> 01:13:16.180
- Oh, it's about holding
people accountable,

01:13:16.180 --> 01:13:20.050
so yes, what I had proposed
is just one piece of this,

01:13:20.050 --> 01:13:21.470
and I have ideas

01:13:21.470 --> 01:13:25.277
for a way to rethink

01:13:27.120 --> 01:13:28.940
large parts of our system now

01:13:28.940 --> 01:13:32.020
where we tend to put responsibility

01:13:32.020 --> 01:13:34.160
very heavily on elected leaders,

01:13:34.160 --> 01:13:36.090
but if you were to take something,

01:13:36.090 --> 01:13:38.630
for example, like the impeachment process,

01:13:38.630 --> 01:13:40.650
I mean, right now, we have
an impeachment process

01:13:40.650 --> 01:13:43.803
that is highly political,

01:13:45.452 --> 01:13:47.520
and, you know, really biased,

01:13:47.520 --> 01:13:50.010
and I don't think very many people

01:13:50.010 --> 01:13:51.830
are all that happy with it,

01:13:51.830 --> 01:13:54.810
but the idea that you
would have some mechanism

01:13:54.810 --> 01:13:56.800
that under a certain threshold,

01:13:56.800 --> 01:13:59.960
whether it's a certain set
of working groups of people

01:13:59.960 --> 01:14:04.960
or a certain say we wanna
start an impeachment process,

01:14:05.370 --> 01:14:08.240
and again, call together working groups

01:14:08.240 --> 01:14:10.370
where you draw random people,

01:14:10.370 --> 01:14:12.860
and it could be a larger
deliberative body,

01:14:12.860 --> 01:14:14.970
I mean, there are lots of ways to do it,

01:14:14.970 --> 01:14:19.630
but again, I think one of
the core bases to my idea

01:14:19.630 --> 01:14:24.630
is that average people
tend to be fairly honest,

01:14:24.940 --> 01:14:27.000
and as long as they're
not part of the system

01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:30.670
and compromised by political
loyalties and things like that,

01:14:30.670 --> 01:14:33.190
they will not do a perfect job,

01:14:33.190 --> 01:14:36.320
but just doing a basically decent job

01:14:36.320 --> 01:14:39.280
is better than what we
have, in many ways, now,

01:14:39.280 --> 01:14:44.190
and that also bringing people together

01:14:44.190 --> 01:14:47.240
with a specific deliberative focus

01:14:47.240 --> 01:14:51.090
allows people to invest
the time and energy

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:52.850
to reach better conclusions

01:14:52.850 --> 01:14:55.810
than the kind of very superficial way

01:14:55.810 --> 01:14:57.220
that people form opinions

01:14:57.220 --> 01:15:01.440
based on mass media or
whatever you have now,

01:15:01.440 --> 01:15:06.020
so I think there are lots of
ways to include working groups,

01:15:06.020 --> 01:15:08.220
various different deliberative bodies

01:15:08.220 --> 01:15:09.827
to, you know, whether it's choosing,

01:15:09.827 --> 01:15:13.040
you know, what do we want the
policy on immigration to be?

01:15:13.040 --> 01:15:15.200
We could, again, put together some sort

01:15:15.200 --> 01:15:19.800
of statistically valid

01:15:19.800 --> 01:15:22.780
deliberative body to
then answer that question

01:15:22.780 --> 01:15:24.820
and give the administrators,

01:15:24.820 --> 01:15:27.270
you know, say, implement that
policy that's been chosen,

01:15:27.270 --> 01:15:29.410
so I think there a lot of different ways

01:15:29.410 --> 01:15:33.180
to reconfigure things to
both get democratic intent

01:15:33.180 --> 01:15:35.223
and to get smarter decision-making.

01:15:36.130 --> 01:15:37.480
- Okay, great, thanks.

01:15:37.480 --> 01:15:40.063
Next up, we're calling on Matthew Meyers.

01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:45.850
- [Matthew] Hi, I'm undergraduate

01:15:45.850 --> 01:15:48.299
in Professor Landemore's class,

01:15:48.299 --> 01:15:52.923
and I have a question for Professor Celis.

01:15:55.290 --> 01:15:58.350
So I think it's really interesting

01:15:58.350 --> 01:16:02.800
to compare sort of your version
of multi-winner elections

01:16:02.800 --> 01:16:05.650
to Mr. Richie's version,

01:16:05.650 --> 01:16:07.270
and I was wondering if,

01:16:07.270 --> 01:16:10.160
when designing the version in Switzerland,

01:16:10.160 --> 01:16:13.000
you considered single transferable vote?

01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:16.600
Because it would sort of have those,

01:16:16.600 --> 01:16:17.433
like, you've had quotas,

01:16:17.433 --> 01:16:19.510
but that would sort of do it built-in

01:16:19.510 --> 01:16:21.770
and incorporate other
elements like ideology

01:16:21.770 --> 01:16:23.570
that aren't so easily categorizable.

01:16:25.520 --> 01:16:27.720
- Yeah, thank you so
much for your question,

01:16:28.786 --> 01:16:29.619
that's very true,

01:16:29.619 --> 01:16:32.490
so these other types of systems,

01:16:32.490 --> 01:16:34.390
like single transferable vote,

01:16:34.390 --> 01:16:38.640
end up with much more
representative committees,

01:16:38.640 --> 01:16:39.960
I was thinking of it
in terms of committees,

01:16:39.960 --> 01:16:44.250
but panels or groups of people.

01:16:44.250 --> 01:16:48.560
It does it via a very different mechanism,

01:16:48.560 --> 01:16:51.180
and I think both approaches

01:16:51.180 --> 01:16:52.910
have different strengths and weaknesses,

01:16:52.910 --> 01:16:55.470
and which you might wanna use

01:16:55.470 --> 01:16:57.223
in a given context would matter,

01:16:58.220 --> 01:17:02.450
I think you absolutely
identified the benefit

01:17:02.450 --> 01:17:05.750
of a single transferable
vote method in that it's,

01:17:05.750 --> 01:17:08.753
you don't need these specific or rigid.

01:17:12.450 --> 01:17:14.160
- Yeah, it's frozen,

01:17:14.160 --> 01:17:17.803
so we'll hope that Elisa
comes back in a second.

01:17:19.580 --> 01:17:20.640
Let me see-
- I might just add

01:17:20.640 --> 01:17:22.060
a comment on that,

01:17:22.060 --> 01:17:24.970
just because I'll be intrigued
to get more of the specifics

01:17:24.970 --> 01:17:26.890
of what happened in Switzerland,

01:17:26.890 --> 01:17:30.330
but there are ways to combine
single transferable vote

01:17:30.330 --> 01:17:33.400
with some kind of guaranteed outcomes

01:17:33.400 --> 01:17:36.233
by gender or other categories,

01:17:37.130 --> 01:17:38.510
and it's an intriguing opportunity

01:17:38.510 --> 01:17:40.510
that actually single
transferable vote permits

01:17:40.510 --> 01:17:41.760
within a proportional system,

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:43.660
so anyway, I'll be interested in hearing

01:17:43.660 --> 01:17:45.820
more about the way it was
done there in Switzerland.

01:17:45.820 --> 01:17:47.563
- Yeah, Elisa, you froze briefly,

01:17:48.420 --> 01:17:49.793
so let's go again.

01:17:51.310 --> 01:17:52.510
- Yeah, so sorry about that,

01:17:52.510 --> 01:17:56.110
my internet cut out and I
have no idea what you heard

01:17:56.110 --> 01:17:57.330
or what you didn't hear,

01:17:57.330 --> 01:17:58.890
so I'll try to not,

01:17:58.890 --> 01:18:00.890
hopefully, this isn't
all repetition for you.

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:04.810
What I was saying is
that it's both systems

01:18:04.810 --> 01:18:06.860
are approaching the same problem,

01:18:06.860 --> 01:18:11.070
like, how do we get a
representative electoral body,

01:18:11.070 --> 01:18:12.690
through very different mechanisms,

01:18:12.690 --> 01:18:15.840
and what I was saying is
that which one you might want

01:18:15.840 --> 01:18:17.380
in a particular context would vary,

01:18:17.380 --> 01:18:20.530
so I don't think one is
strictly better than the other.

01:18:20.530 --> 01:18:24.026
The difference is kind of
the mechanism that is used

01:18:24.026 --> 01:18:27.100
and results when a
single transferable vote,

01:18:27.100 --> 01:18:29.450
what I was saying, as Matthew
very clearly identified,

01:18:29.450 --> 01:18:32.090
that one of the huge benefits

01:18:32.090 --> 01:18:35.770
is that it kind of implicitly
gets this diversification

01:18:35.770 --> 01:18:38.450
and doesn't need these
rigid separate categories

01:18:38.450 --> 01:18:40.810
as to how many women

01:18:40.810 --> 01:18:43.010
or how many people from
such-and-such category,

01:18:43.010 --> 01:18:44.920
and I think that is a huge value

01:18:44.920 --> 01:18:45.980
because it does allow you

01:18:45.980 --> 01:18:49.380
to get all these other
more implicit categories.

01:18:49.380 --> 01:18:52.303
I would say the downside is that,

01:18:53.660 --> 01:18:56.860
while overall, you get
more diverse committees

01:18:56.860 --> 01:18:59.340
or more representation,

01:18:59.340 --> 01:19:00.610
it's not guaranteed,

01:19:00.610 --> 01:19:02.810
and if you have groups of people

01:19:02.810 --> 01:19:07.560
that are very underrepresented
in the overall population

01:19:07.560 --> 01:19:10.610
but still deserve, rightly, a voice,

01:19:10.610 --> 01:19:12.680
it generally is not enough

01:19:12.680 --> 01:19:15.590
to get enough votes on
those candidates, right?

01:19:15.590 --> 01:19:16.730
Or it might not be enough,

01:19:16.730 --> 01:19:18.980
depending on the particular context,

01:19:18.980 --> 01:19:22.453
so by actually setting
up these strict rules,

01:19:23.890 --> 01:19:25.410
you lose something in that brittleness,

01:19:25.410 --> 01:19:28.410
but you also gain something
by first having a guarantee

01:19:28.410 --> 01:19:29.560
that you're actually going to get

01:19:29.560 --> 01:19:31.140
that desired representation

01:19:31.140 --> 01:19:33.720
and making sure that all
parts of your population

01:19:33.720 --> 01:19:34.763
are represented.

01:19:36.890 --> 01:19:37.723
- Thank you.

01:19:39.500 --> 01:19:42.283
Shyam Sunder, is Shyam there?

01:19:49.700 --> 01:19:51.010
- [Shyam] Can you hear me?

01:19:51.010 --> 01:19:51.870
- Yes.

01:19:51.870 --> 01:19:53.320
- [Shyam] Okay.

01:19:53.320 --> 01:19:57.680
So the behavior of elected representatives

01:19:58.880 --> 01:20:00.960
has long been debated,

01:20:00.960 --> 01:20:04.460
what they actually choose to do

01:20:04.460 --> 01:20:06.083
or whether they represent,

01:20:07.112 --> 01:20:08.930
they actually behave in a way

01:20:08.930 --> 01:20:12.680
that represents the constituent interests

01:20:12.680 --> 01:20:16.350
or the assessment of a general will,

01:20:16.350 --> 01:20:18.610
a general good for society,

01:20:18.610 --> 01:20:21.263
or something else, an
interest of their own,

01:20:22.290 --> 01:20:27.290
my question is, how do you
think your respective proposals

01:20:28.540 --> 01:20:33.523
might affect the behaviors
of elected representatives?

01:20:37.420 --> 01:20:38.560
- Okay, we'll call on people

01:20:38.560 --> 01:20:41.020
in the order in which they present

01:20:42.160 --> 01:20:43.030
if they would like to speak,

01:20:43.030 --> 01:20:45.480
and if they don't, they don't have to,

01:20:45.480 --> 01:20:46.313
so Elisa.

01:20:48.000 --> 01:20:53.000
- Yes, so I think there's a
explicit way in which it changes

01:20:53.000 --> 01:20:57.400
in that if you have a more
diverse representative body,

01:20:57.400 --> 01:21:01.590
you just, by nature, have to
tackle problems differently

01:21:01.590 --> 01:21:04.740
because you can't conveniently ignore

01:21:04.740 --> 01:21:06.120
a huge part of the population

01:21:06.120 --> 01:21:07.870
you're supposed to be representing,

01:21:08.770 --> 01:21:12.190
but I think the other effect that we saw

01:21:12.190 --> 01:21:13.790
when doing this in Switzerland

01:21:13.790 --> 01:21:18.730
was that it was by far the
most diverse set of candidates

01:21:18.730 --> 01:21:21.800
that we had seen in several years,

01:21:21.800 --> 01:21:25.070
so I think part of what implicitly happens

01:21:25.070 --> 01:21:28.330
when you set up alternative
systems of governance

01:21:28.330 --> 01:21:29.163
is that you (indistinct)

01:21:29.163 --> 01:21:31.270
people who might otherwise
be disillusioned,

01:21:31.270 --> 01:21:33.667
who might otherwise say,
"Okay, there's no way I have

01:21:33.667 --> 01:21:35.720
"any chance of winning this election,"

01:21:35.720 --> 01:21:37.310
they participate as well,

01:21:37.310 --> 01:21:39.400
so that's one way in which,

01:21:39.400 --> 01:21:41.610
kind of both explicitly and implicitly,

01:21:41.610 --> 01:21:43.520
the conversation shifts,

01:21:43.520 --> 01:21:45.900
and you end up getting people

01:21:45.900 --> 01:21:49.010
with different interests, perhaps,

01:21:49.010 --> 01:21:51.620
or different motivations to be there

01:21:51.620 --> 01:21:54.463
and to be part of the process
than you would otherwise have.

01:21:56.650 --> 01:21:58.990
- Yeah, I think it's a very good question,

01:21:58.990 --> 01:22:03.990
and this, the problem of politicians

01:22:04.440 --> 01:22:09.440
being motivated by their own
interests has really been,

01:22:09.690 --> 01:22:11.650
you know, is kind of a very important,

01:22:11.650 --> 01:22:15.963
in my thinking as I try
to design my system,

01:22:17.900 --> 01:22:19.820
I mean, because at least right now,

01:22:19.820 --> 01:22:22.860
and again, my experiences
are very much New York-based,

01:22:22.860 --> 01:22:24.710
but I think that they're,

01:22:24.710 --> 01:22:28.500
what we see here is common elsewhere,

01:22:28.500 --> 01:22:32.940
but the dominant political
forces in New York are unions,

01:22:32.940 --> 01:22:37.840
real estate interests, the
government contractors,

01:22:37.840 --> 01:22:39.990
and if you look at how the system works,

01:22:39.990 --> 01:22:42.830
it's basically to benefit those interests

01:22:42.830 --> 01:22:46.940
because those interests are able to pay,

01:22:46.940 --> 01:22:49.680
directly or indirectly or
through blocks of votes

01:22:49.680 --> 01:22:52.690
or things like that,
the various politicians,

01:22:52.690 --> 01:22:55.640
and the politicians are
acting in their interest

01:22:55.640 --> 01:22:57.040
and not the interest of the public,

01:22:57.040 --> 01:22:59.513
and I think you see that all over,

01:23:00.860 --> 01:23:04.490
so I very much tried to create a system

01:23:04.490 --> 01:23:09.140
where you don't have
these outside interests

01:23:09.140 --> 01:23:13.990
having very much direct sway

01:23:13.990 --> 01:23:17.700
over the government officials,

01:23:17.700 --> 01:23:21.770
the flip side of that is that
there is less accountability,

01:23:21.770 --> 01:23:24.660
and so that is why I do
think it is important

01:23:24.660 --> 01:23:26.260
if we were to adopt my system

01:23:26.260 --> 01:23:29.140
to have these sort of parallel systems,

01:23:29.140 --> 01:23:31.760
which I talked about with sort
of separate working groups

01:23:31.760 --> 01:23:35.193
to provide some sort of
accountability there,

01:23:36.100 --> 01:23:37.260
but at least for me,

01:23:37.260 --> 01:23:41.890
this idea of interest of the politicians

01:23:41.890 --> 01:23:43.210
is really core to everything

01:23:43.210 --> 01:23:47.420
because, at least for me, it
doesn't necessarily matter

01:23:47.420 --> 01:23:51.140
what a particular government
official looks like

01:23:51.140 --> 01:23:52.820
or which group they represent

01:23:52.820 --> 01:23:54.920
if they're really just
representing themselves

01:23:54.920 --> 01:23:57.900
and whatever interest is
capable of buying them off,

01:23:57.900 --> 01:24:02.900
and so I think this idea
of minimizing corruption

01:24:02.960 --> 01:24:05.490
is a completely parallel set of issues

01:24:05.490 --> 01:24:08.923
in addition to increasing
diversity and representation.

01:24:09.810 --> 01:24:11.510
- Thank you. Helene?

01:24:11.510 --> 01:24:13.793
- Yes, I think it's a
really good question,

01:24:13.793 --> 01:24:14.767
I don't know how to answer,

01:24:14.767 --> 01:24:19.373
but my suspicion is that it
really realign their behavior

01:24:19.373 --> 01:24:23.080
with the common interests
and the public interest,

01:24:23.080 --> 01:24:24.430
at least to a degree,

01:24:24.430 --> 01:24:27.400
because what I observed,
even in the context

01:24:27.400 --> 01:24:29.630
of the French Citizens'
Convention for Climate,

01:24:29.630 --> 01:24:32.630
which was, you know, purely advisory,

01:24:32.630 --> 01:24:36.310
is that they had, the
parliamentarians had to,

01:24:36.310 --> 01:24:40.710
and the government had to
take into account this input

01:24:40.710 --> 01:24:43.370
and it had to justify themselves publicly

01:24:43.370 --> 01:24:45.460
as to why they ignore certain proposals,

01:24:45.460 --> 01:24:48.440
and that creates a form of
deliberative accountability

01:24:49.563 --> 01:24:51.800
that is then, you know, these
arguments are mediatized

01:24:51.800 --> 01:24:53.933
and journalists can press questions,

01:24:55.364 --> 01:24:58.460
it becomes harder for the
elected parliamentarians

01:24:58.460 --> 01:25:00.380
to stay stuck in partisan logics

01:25:00.380 --> 01:25:02.740
and ignore what the larger public

01:25:02.740 --> 01:25:06.260
represented by this
mini-public arguably wants,

01:25:06.260 --> 01:25:07.940
so I think it's for the better.

01:25:09.220 --> 01:25:10.053
- Rob?

01:25:11.660 --> 01:25:14.110
- So I think the questioner

01:25:14.110 --> 01:25:16.190
gets at sort of what
the organizing principle

01:25:16.190 --> 01:25:17.200
of my presentation was,

01:25:17.200 --> 01:25:18.710
that we have to change incentives,

01:25:18.710 --> 01:25:20.560
and I think these two proposals do.

01:25:20.560 --> 01:25:23.340
Give a couple little
examples for each one,

01:25:23.340 --> 01:25:24.770
so the ranked-choice ballot,

01:25:24.770 --> 01:25:27.610
by just giving people backup preferences,

01:25:27.610 --> 01:25:29.730
just means that you now, as a candidate,

01:25:29.730 --> 01:25:32.250
have this different incentive
to talk to more people,

01:25:32.250 --> 01:25:33.330
you particularly see this,

01:25:33.330 --> 01:25:36.530
again, in nonpartisan
or primary elections,

01:25:36.530 --> 01:25:40.710
you will see regularly
the winners say, "Gosh,"

01:25:40.710 --> 01:25:41.980
or losers too,

01:25:41.980 --> 01:25:43.087
you know, "this was really interesting,

01:25:43.087 --> 01:25:44.827
"I mean, I just threw out the playbook

01:25:44.827 --> 01:25:47.757
"that you just talk to
your base and swing voters

01:25:47.757 --> 01:25:49.023
"and talked to everybody

01:25:49.023 --> 01:25:50.680
"and just looked for a connection," right?

01:25:50.680 --> 01:25:52.290
Which is what you want, I think,

01:25:52.290 --> 01:25:54.320
and that just comes from,

01:25:54.320 --> 01:25:56.230
the voter knows that they now have

01:25:56.230 --> 01:25:57.750
a second or third choice
that could matter,

01:25:57.750 --> 01:26:00.080
and so they need to think
more about candidates,

01:26:00.080 --> 01:26:02.390
and the candidate knows that,

01:26:02.390 --> 01:26:04.370
and then, ultimately, as a representative

01:26:04.370 --> 01:26:06.580
just has learned a lot
more about the people there

01:26:06.580 --> 01:26:07.800
that they're gonna represent.

01:26:07.800 --> 01:26:08.810
Not the be all and end all,

01:26:08.810 --> 01:26:11.600
but a clearly just sort of step forward.

01:26:11.600 --> 01:26:12.760
The multi-member district,

01:26:12.760 --> 01:26:15.853
particularly with a proportional system,

01:26:16.964 --> 01:26:18.370
and these are these candidate-based,

01:26:18.370 --> 01:26:21.230
American-style kind of forms
of proportional voting,

01:26:21.230 --> 01:26:22.863
we've had some examples of them,

01:26:24.860 --> 01:26:27.640
the two really kind of
clearest differences

01:26:27.640 --> 01:26:32.610
are that every representative
will share constituents

01:26:32.610 --> 01:26:34.570
with someone from a different party,

01:26:34.570 --> 01:26:35.700
so you have a different reason

01:26:35.700 --> 01:26:38.160
when you're sort of
serving your constituency

01:26:38.160 --> 01:26:39.750
to talk with people of another party

01:26:39.750 --> 01:26:42.410
because you actually
share a representative,

01:26:42.410 --> 01:26:44.410
you know, you share constituents,

01:26:44.410 --> 01:26:47.530
and when the parties
retreat to their caucuses,

01:26:47.530 --> 01:26:48.690
they kind of bring the whole country

01:26:48.690 --> 01:26:51.870
or the whole state into the room,

01:26:51.870 --> 01:26:55.440
and the interests of cities
are part of Republican caucuses

01:26:55.440 --> 01:26:58.100
and of rural areas are part
of Democratic caucuses,

01:26:58.100 --> 01:27:01.290
and so the attentiveness of both caucuses,

01:27:01.290 --> 01:27:02.140
major parties at least,

01:27:02.140 --> 01:27:05.370
to be kind of thinking about
the whole country changes,

01:27:05.370 --> 01:27:06.680
and of course, the ranked-choice feature

01:27:06.680 --> 01:27:09.760
kind of creates different opportunities

01:27:09.760 --> 01:27:13.830
to be rewarded for sort
of solving problems

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:16.360
for at least some representatives,

01:27:16.360 --> 01:27:18.090
that's what they will be in office for,

01:27:18.090 --> 01:27:19.990
and they can get reelected to do that.

01:27:21.390 --> 01:27:23.270
- So thanks to all the panelists

01:27:23.270 --> 01:27:26.620
for offering fresh ideas and insights

01:27:26.620 --> 01:27:28.530
on a perennial question,

01:27:28.530 --> 01:27:32.290
how do we get better representation

01:27:32.290 --> 01:27:35.000
and, hopefully, better outcomes?

